DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers Herder's distinctive contributions to hermeneutics
and the methodology of historical understanding, centered on three
interrelated problems: (1) the concept of Einfühlung (empathetic
feeling-into) as an epistemic tool for grasping alien cultures and
historical epochs; (2) the philosophical grounding of this method in
Herder's theory of language and mind, especially as worked out in the
Treatise on the Origin of Language (1772); and (3) the claim that
each historical epoch carries its "standard within itself" (jede
Nation hat ihren Mittelpunkt in sich), which raises the tension
between cultural self-sufficiency and developmental narrative. The
secondary literature divides broadly between reconstructive accounts
that foreground Herder as a systematic thinker of interpretation
(Forster's multi-volume project), critical accounts that situate him
within Enlightenment anthropological and poetic discourse (Gjesdal),
and historical surveys that assess Herder's role in shaping the
subsequent hermeneutic tradition through Schleiermacher, Dilthey, and
Gadamer. A subordinate but important debate concerns whether Einfühlung
is epistemologically coherent or leads to relativism, reductionism,
or mere projection (Reichert, Wildschut).

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Herder's hermeneutics constitutes a foundational node in any
comprehensive account of his philosophy of history: his claim that
historical epochs are internally self-measuring wholes is
simultaneously a historiographical thesis and a hermeneutical one,
requiring the interpreter to adopt a methodologically alien standpoint.
The question of how Einfühlung relates to his language philosophy —
whether language is the medium through which Verstehen operates — is
central to assessing both the coherence and the legacy of his position.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The relationship between Herder's Einfühlung and Gadamer's critique
of empathy-based hermeneutics (in Truth and Method) deserves more
sustained attention than most secondary literature provides; Gjesdal's
article and Nassar's chapter begin to fill this gap but do not resolve
the interpretive question. Primary texts by Herder that are not yet
available in critical English translation remain an obstacle for
anglophone reception.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The synthesis should map the debate between Forster (Herder as
systematic founder of modern hermeneutics) and Gjesdal (Herder as
Enlightenment anthropological thinker) as the central organizing
axis, and situate the other entries in relation to that debate.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Herder as founder of modern hermeneutics (Forster): 4 papers
- Herder within Enlightenment anthropological/poetic discourse (Gjesdal): 2 papers
- Einfühlung: epistemology and limits: 3 papers
- Historical reception (Schleiermacher, Dilthey, Gadamer): 3 papers
- Philosophy of language as ground of hermeneutics: 3 papers
====================================================================
}

