  number    = {4},
  pages     = {527--537},
  doi       = {10.2307/2708100},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Identifies a fundamental tension between Herder's two major historical works: the Auch eine Philosophie (1774) is presented as relativist and anti-teleological, celebrating each epoch's intrinsic worth and rejecting Enlightenment progressivism, while the Ideen (1784-91) advances a developmental teleology with Humanitaet as the directional goal of all history. Wells argues these represent genuinely different philosophical commitments rather than a single coherent position.

  RELEVANCE: Sets the terms for subsequent debate about whether Herder held a coherent philosophy of history; the "two philosophies" thesis remains the orienting problem for much subsequent scholarship (Bollacher, James, Bristow) and is directly relevant to how the project characterizes Herder's teleology.

  POSITION: Critical reading that stresses inconsistency rather than tension-within-unity; subsequent scholarship has largely tried to refute or modulate this verdict.
  },
  keywords  = {Auch-eine-Philosophie, Ideen, teleology, relativism, historicism, High},
  abstract = {Both Stadelmann and Meinecke consider Herder's early philosophy of history, the Auch eine Philosophie der Geschichte, written in Buckeburg in 1774, to be a greater achievement than the Ideen zur Philosophie der Geschichte der Menschheit of his Weimar period, published in four volumes between 1784 and 1791. I propose in this paper to examine the reasons they give for their preference. My justification for doing so is, first, that they are regarded as important authorities on Herder, and second, that their methods of evaluating his typify an approach to history and historians common today. Stadelmann 'considers that since Herder holds all historical phenomena to be determined by their antecedents, he is not entitled to criticize the medieval clergy and aristocracy for their behavior; but that this is what he does in the Ideen, whereas in the early Auch eine Philosophie he was much more detached and felt his way into the past instead of passing judgment on it. In the Ideen, he says, Herder's intuitive genial [kongenial] understanding for the religious and aristocratic greatness of the Middle Ages is killed by strong liberal-republican and Protestant antipathy to the aristocratic constitution that suppressed the industry and inventiveness of the artisans, and to the tyranny the clergy exercised over men's minds. He holds that the Auch eine Philosophie is free from this defect and that Herder begins to lose his detachment only after his move to Weimar.1 This is an extraordinary statement, especially as Stadelmann himself admits that the Auch eine Philosophie is a virulent attack on modern decadence-an attack effected by boosting the medieval past. Herder, he says,  plays off the Middle Ages against modern times as heart against head, achievement against morbid reflection, firm-rootedness against dissipation  (23). If the criticism of the medieval world in the Ideen constitutes a defect in that work, then the criticism of the present in the Auch eine Philosophie is a like blemish, and later in his book Stadelmann admits that this is so:  In the Biickeburg paper Herder abandons his universal-historical standpoint when he comes to speak of the present  (89). So the criterion of detachment is, after all, useless as a means of evaluating the two works, and Stadelmann sets to work to find another: for of the fact he has no doubt:  The Humanititsbriefe and even the Ideen are further removed from true historical sense than Herder's youthful works (57). The following passage is an attempt to justify this view:},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mertel2014historicism,
  author    = {Mertel, Kurt C. M.},
  title     = {Historicism and Critique in {H}erder's \textit{Another Philosophy of History}: Some Hermeneutic Reflections},
  journal   = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year      = {2014},
  volume    = {24},
  number    = {2},
  pages     = {397--416},
  doi       = {10.1111/ejop.12088},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Herder's stated project in the Auch eine Philosophie -- merely describing each culture without comparative judgment -- is undermined by a tension between his doctrine of historicism and cultural relativism on the one hand, and his actual practice of critique on the other. Drawing on Gadamer's philosophical hermeneutics, Mertel shows that understanding presupposes justification and that prejudice is enabling rather than merely distorting, which discloses two conditions of possibility for Herder's project of which Herder himself was unaware.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the Auch eine Philosophie and the question of whether historicism and critique are compatible in Herder; relevant to how the project frames Herder's methodology and the tensions in his historical relativism.

  POSITION: Gadamerian reading that reconstructs Herder's practice as more coherent than his stated doctrine, using hermeneutic philosophy to resolve what appeared as contradiction.
  },
  keywords  = {Auch-eine-Philosophie, historicism, hermeneutics, Gadamer, relativism, High},
  abstract = {Abstract In Another Philosophy of History , J . G . H erder claims that his aim is not to compare and judge different cultures, but merely to describe and explain how each came into being and thus to adopt the standpoint of an impartial observer. I argue, however, that there is a tension between H erder's understanding of his own project—his stated doctrine of historicism and cultural relativism—and the way in which it is actually put into practice. That is, despite H erder's stated aims, he is nevertheless unable to avoid justifying premodern forms of life and making context‐transcending evaluative judgments in the process of trying to understand cultures on their own terms and holding them up as exemplars vis à vis the Enlightenment. This tension presents the challenge of accounting for it in the most charitable and illuminating way. I argue that this goal can be achieved by appealing to the resources of the philosophical hermeneutics of H ans‐ G eorg G adamer, which enables us to disclose two enabling conditions for H erder's project of which he was not explicitly aware, viz., the internal connection between understanding and justification and the enabling character of prejudice as the condition for the possibility of understanding as such.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{james2022situating,
  author    = {James, David},
  title     = {Situating the {E}nlightenment in {H}erder's Philosophy of History},
  journal   = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year      = {2022},
  volume    = {16},
  number    = {3},
  pages     = {247--270},
  doi       = {10.1163/18722636-12341480},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that abstract thinking and hostility to prejudice -- features Herder famously criticizes in Enlightenment thought -- are themselves integral to Herder's project of situating the Enlightenment within his philosophy of history. The Enlightenment makes a distinctive contribution to humanity's development, but this cannot make it superior to prior ages, since each age has equal value as a contribution to the full development of Humanitaet.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the core tension between Herder's critique of Enlightenment progressivism and his own commitment to a teleological conception of Humanitaet; provides a careful recent reconstruction of how Herder reconciles the intrinsic worth of each period with a weak universal telos.

  POSITION: Defends a coherentist reading against Wells's two-philosophies thesis; Herder's universalism and his particularism are mutually constraining rather than contradictory.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, teleology, Enlightenment, Ideen, pluralism, High},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 Although Herder is critical of the Enlightenment, I show that his philosophy of history commits him to the claim that the age and culture shaped by the Enlightenment in some way makes a distinctive contribution to the development of humanity. Yet this contribution cannot make this age and culture superior to earlier ones, for this would violate Herder’s commitment to the principle that each age and culture ought to be accorded an equal status because of the equal value of its contribution to the full development of humanity. I argue that an abstract way of thinking and a hostility to prejudice are integral elements of Herder’s attempt to situate the Enlightenment in his philosophy of history, despite how he otherwise criticizes the Enlightenment for its abstract thinking and its hostility to prejudice.},
}

@article{bristow2025herder,
  author    = {Bristow, William},
  title     = {Herder, Philosophy and Universal History},
  journal   = {The European Legacy},
  year      = {2025},
  volume    = {30},
  number    = {6},
  pages     = {712--727},
  doi       = {10.1080/10848770.2025.2525597},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the philosophical status of Herder's claim that history has a universal direction toward Humanitaet, arguing that this universalism is philosophically grounded and not merely rhetorical, but that it is structured so as to be compatible with the irreducible individuality and worth of particular peoples and epochs.

  RELEVANCE: The most recent sustained treatment of Herder's philosophy of universal history; directly engages the project's question of whether Humanitaet is a single goal or a regulative idea compatible with plural ends.

  POSITION: Moderate universalist reading that seeks to vindicate Herder's teleological claims without sacrificing his commitment to cultural particularity.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, universal-history, teleology, pluralism, High},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT In this article I propose that the tension between two of Herder’s most important contributions to Western thought and culture, the incomparability thesis and the history thesis, is reconciled by Herder’s own innovative project and method of universal history. Whereas the incomparability thesis demands that peoples are known in their uniqueness without comparison to other peoples, the history thesis implies knowing peoples in relation to each other as distinct parts of a universal history of humanity. I interpret Herder’s method and project of universal history as a precursor of Hegel’s project and method of his Phenomenology of Spirit. The comparison between Herder’s and Hegel’s methods and projects shows that Herder’s universal history is a philosophical inquiry—empirical, critical, skeptical, and open-ended—into the meaning and telos of human existence. I propose in concluding that Herder’s innovative practice of philosophy as universal history is a third significant contribution to European thought.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{gjesdal2017herder,
  author    = {Gjesdal, Kristin},
  title     = {Herder's Hermeneutics: History, Poetry, Enlightenment},
  year      = {2017},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi       = {10.1017/9781316285770},
  abstract  = {Herder's Hermeneutics offers a comprehensive study of Herder's theory of understanding as applied to history, poetry, and the Enlightenment. Gjesdal argues that Herder develops a non-essentialist approach to art, history, and reason that constitutes a systematic alternative to both Hegel's essentialist historicism and later Romantic subjectivism.},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Herder develops a philosophically coherent hermeneutics grounded in historical situatedness, empathetic understanding (Einfuehlung), and attention to poetic form that anticipates later nineteenth-century hermeneutics but is irreducible to it. Reconstructs how Herder's historical method is shaped by his engagement with biblical criticism, classical philology, and Enlightenment debates about taste.

  RELEVANCE: Most thorough recent account of the hermeneutic and methodological foundations of Herder's historical thinking; directly relevant to the question of what Herder means by understanding a historical period "from within" and how this relates to his genetic method.

  POSITION: Positions Herder as offering a non-essentialist alternative to Hegel that merits rehabilitation on its own terms rather than as a precursor to later thought.
  },
  keywords  = {hermeneutics, Einfuehlung, historical-method, Ideen, Enlightenment, High}
}

@book{sikka2011herder,
  author    = {Sikka, Sonia},
  title     = {Herder on Humanity and Cultural Difference: Enlightened Relativism},
  year      = {2011},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi       = {10.1017/CBO9780511783012},
  abstract  = {Examines Herder's cultural relativism in its philosophical context, arguing that Herder's relativism is consistent with a commitment to Humanitaet as a universal value. Analyses the relationship between cultural difference, human nature, and historical development across Herder's major works.},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Herder's relativism is not inconsistent with his commitment to Humanitaet: the latter functions as a thin universal value that each culture realizes differently rather than as a substantive norm against which cultures are measured. Cultural difference is positively valued as the vehicle through which humanity expresses its full range of capacities.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the tension between pluralism and universalism that is central to the project; argues for a coherent reading of Herder's teleology that respects both poles.

  POSITION: Defends "enlightened relativism" as Herder's considered view: relativism about evaluative standards combined with universalism about human nature and dignity.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, relativism, cultural-difference, teleology, High}
}

@book{barnard2003herder,
  author    = {Barnard, F.M.},
  title     = {Herder on Nationality, Humanity, and History},
  year      = {2003},
  publisher = {McGill-Queen's University Press},
  doi       = {10.1515/9780773570917},
  abstract  = {Synthesizes Barnard's decades of work on Herder's political and historical thought, examining the relationships among nationality, humanity, and historical development. Argues that Herder's philosophy of history is structured around the tension between the particularity of national communities and the universality of human nature.},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Synthesizes Barnard's extensive work on Herder to argue that nationality and humanity are not competing values but complementary poles in Herder's thought: particular nations are the organic units through which universal human capacities are developed and expressed. History is the process through which this double articulation unfolds.

  RELEVANCE: Barnard is the most important Anglophone scholar of Herder's political and historical thought; this volume provides the fullest account of how Herder reconciles nationalism, cultural particularity, and universal humanity -- the very tension the project needs to address.

  POSITION: Reconciliationist reading that treats Herder as holding a coherent philosophy in which organic nationality is the necessary vehicle of Humanitaet.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, nationality, organic-development, Ideen, historicism, High}
}

@article{nassar2015analogy,
  author = {Nassar, Dalia},
  title = {Analogy, Natural History and the Philosophy of Nature: Kant, Herder and the Organisation of Nature},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {9},
  number = {2},
  pages = {240--257},
  doi = {10.1163/18722636-12341302},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nassar examines how Herder and Kant deploy analogical
  reasoning differently in their accounts of organic nature and history.
  Herder uses analogy to establish a continuum between organic wholes and
  historical cultures, arguing for a naturalistic teleology that is built
  into the fabric of things, while Kant insists that teleological judgments
  are merely regulative. This divergence is central to understanding Kant's
  critique of the Ideen's methodology.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a precise philosophical account of the methodological
  disagreement between Herder and Kant over teleology that underlies Kant's
  reviews of the Ideen. Essential for understanding what is at stake in the
  controversy over natural history and philosophy of nature.

  POSITION: The Kant-Herder dispute over teleology is fundamentally about
  the status of analogical reasoning in natural and historical inquiry.
  },
  keywords = {teleology, analogy, natural history, Kant, Herder, Ideen, High},
  abstract = {In 1785 Kant published a series of critical reviews of Johann Gottfried Herder’s Ideas for a Philosophy of the History of Humanity (1784–1785), in which he not only challenges Herder’s conception of nature but also, and more importantly, his methodology. Kant’s complaint is that by relying on analogy, Herder draws deeply mistaken conclusions that overlook fundamental differences between human and nonhuman beings. But was Kant’s critique of Herder entirely fair? And how does it compare to Kant’s own use of analogy? My claim is that Herder’s use of analogy posed a fundamental methodological challenge to Kant, a challenge he sought to meet in the years following the reviews. In so doing, however, Kant found himself in the untenable situation of, on the one hand, granting analogy greater significance, and, on the other, severely restricting its use. By tracing the shifts in Kant’s thought through the lens of analogy, I aim to show that Kant’s transformed understanding of analogy reveals a fundamental tension between his a priori “metaphysics of nature” and empirical science, a tension that fundamentally shaped the philosophies of nature after Kant.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{zammito2009metanarrative,
  author    = {Zammito, John},
  title     = {Herder and Historical Metanarrative: What's Philosophical about History?},
  booktitle = {A Companion to the Works of Johann Gottfried Herder},
  year      = {2009},
  pages     = {65--92},
  publisher = {Boydell and Brewer Limited},
  doi       = {10.1017/9781571137289.004},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that the "father of historicism" label, standardly derived from Meinecke and Stadelmann, involves retrospective redemption: Herder was absorbed into the historicist tradition of Ranke and Droysen by being selectively read. Zammito shows that what Herder himself was doing philosophically -- offering a metanarrative about the purpose and direction of history -- differs substantially from the later positivist historicism that claimed him as founder.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the Meinecke thesis and its problems; provides the most critical account of the "father of historicism" attribution, showing that Herder's providential teleology and metanarrative ambitions are foreign to the disciplinary matrix of nineteenth-century historicism.

  POSITION: Revisionist: challenges the received narrative by showing the discontinuity between Herder's philosophical ambitions and the later historicist tradition.
  },
  keywords  = {historicism, Meinecke, metanarrative, Ranke, providence, Medium},
  abstract = {THERE IS SOME TRUTH in Goethe's pronouncement that Johann Gottfried Herder's ideas had been both absorbed and forgotten by the conventional wisdom of German culture after 1800. He was absorbed either into the project of the Jena Romantiker and their literary hermeneutics or into the project of Hegel and his philosophy of history. But after the rise of historicism in the school of Ranke and Droysen, a retrospective redemption seemed possible. Thus, in the classic formulations of Meinecke and Stadelmann, Herder was resurrected as the "father of historicism." He was credited with pioneering the stress on individuality, development, and the "historical sense" of Einfühlung (or Verstehen [empathy]) that became the core of the disciplinary matrix of history in the nineteenth century.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{bollacher2017individualism,
  author    = {Bollacher, Martin},
  title     = {Individualism and Universalism in {H}erder's Conception of the Philosophy of History},
  booktitle = {Herder: Philosophy and Anthropology},
  editor    = {Waldow, Anik and DeSouza, Nigel},
  year      = {2017},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi       = {10.1093/oso/9780198779650.003.0012},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Uses Herder's "globe ontology" -- the spherical earth as a matrix structuring the contrast between particular peoples and universal humanity -- to argue that Herder's philosophy of history is organized around a productive opposition rather than a contradiction. The individuality of cultures and the universality of human species are mutually constitutive: Humanitaet is realized only through the full diversity of particular peoples, not despite it.

  RELEVANCE: Directly engages the tension between universal telos and particular worth in Herder's Ideen; the globe metaphor provides a concrete image for how Herder holds plurality and universality together without reducing either.

  POSITION: Coherentist reading that frames the individuality-universality tension as a structural feature of Herder's thought rather than an inconsistency to be overcome.
  },
  keywords  = {Humanitaet, universalism, individualism, Ideen, teleology, Medium},
  abstract = {Herder was very familiar with the concept of human rights (in his German, “Menschenrechte”), strongly shared the moral ideals of protecting people at which the concept aims, but tended to avoid using the concept himself. Why this ambivalence? The present article argues that a number of well-founded concerns about the concept underlie, or at least may underlie, his reservation, including concerns about the <italic>legal</italic> rather than <italic>moral</italic> nature of the very concept of “rights,” the undue restriction of the focus of “human rights” to protecting people against threats that emanate from their own governments rather than against those from outside (imperialism, colonialism, etc.) as well, and the concept’s dubious restriction of protections to human beings to the exclusion of animals. The article also argues, however, that these concerns are not insurmountable, and that indeed Herder himself has provided some of the most important theoretical tools for surmounting them.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{iggers1968german,
  author    = {Iggers, Georg G.},
  title     = {The German Conception of History: The National Tradition of Historical Thought from Herder to the Present},
  year      = {1968},
  publisher = {Wesleyan University Press},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Provides the first comprehensive critical examination in English of the German national tradition of historiography from Herder to Meinecke and beyond. Iggers argues that the tradition rests on the Lutheran-historicist thesis that existing institutions have rational grounding in God's will while all values must be understood within historical flux; this led to a distinctively German accommodation of liberal constitutional ideals to authoritarian state structures.

  RELEVANCE: Essential for understanding how Herder's cosmopolitan, culture-oriented nationalism was transformed into the state-centered nationalism of later historicism; contextualizes both the Meinecke thesis and the limits of reading Herder as the simple founder of the tradition.

  POSITION: Critical of the conservative political consequences of historicism; treats Herder's contribution as ambiguous -- his cultural pluralism points away from the statist nationalism that claimed him.
  },
  keywords = {historicism, Meinecke, German-thought, nationalism, Herder, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{berlin1976vico,
  author = {Berlin, Isaiah},
  title = {Vico and Herder: Two Studies in the History of Ideas},
  year = {1976},
  publisher = {Viking Press},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Berlin argues that Herder is the founder of value pluralism—the view that genuine human values are many, irreducibly diverse, and incommensurable with one another—and that this pluralism is compatible with a form of universalism grounded in a shared human nature. On this reading, Herder avoids relativism because the values instantiated in different cultures are authentically human values, not merely parochial preferences.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational text for the Berlin-pluralist interpretation of Herder that has structured all subsequent debate; any account of Herder's philosophy of history must engage with Berlin's thesis about the distinction between pluralism and relativism and his claim that Humanitat supplies a universal human standard.

  POSITION: Berlin's pluralist reading; the locus classicus for attributing value pluralism to Herder.
  },
  keywords = {value-pluralism, Berlin, Herder-interpretation, incommensurability, High},
  abstract = {Nowadays one main narrative approach to the history of German philosophy of the early nineteenth century involves a distinctive version of the 'back to Kant' ethos. The rallying call 'back to Kant' seems to have originated with Otto Liebmann, whoseKant und die Epigonen(1865) expresses well, if not exactly in the first flush of things, what became a general sentiment among scientifically-minded philosophers in the mid- to late-nineteenth century: that philosophy should be cleansed of unseemly idealism by picking up where Kant left off. The phrase 'where Kant left off' is no throwaway; there can be and have been many views concerning the question of precisely where Kant left off. Neo-Kantians like Helmholtz and Lange took advances in the empirical sciences of their day to show that one must deepen one's commitment to key Kantian doctrines; Helmholtz, for instance, held that research in the physiology of perception mandated adopting Kant's view that 'cause and effect' is an a priori concept. Thus the twist: science done from a more or less materialist perspective in fact ends one up in a non-materialist, or at least non-'naïve' materialist, epistemology. Scientific methodology and the epistemology of science properly diverge. Lange'sGeschichte des Materialismus und Kritik seiner Bedeutung in der Gegenwart(1866) puts the point systematically. Of course, in an important sense, to say that materialism is fine as a methodological precept is profoundly un-Kantian -- Kant himself was quite fond of theologically-based regulative constraints on science. But the point was not to preserve every bit of Kant; it was rather to show that the best science ratified the best bits.

The Helmholtz-Lange form of the 'back to Kant' movement left German idealism out in the cold. Indeed, although it was reacting most pointedly to a surge of materialism in the philosophy of physics and biology of its time, putting down idealism was also an explicit and primary aim of Lange's project. Idealism also met direct resistance from materialism in the emerging social sciences, often initiated from within idealism's own ranks. This was the prompt for Benedetto Croce's posing the question of 'what is living and what is dead in the philosophy of Hegel'.[1]Croce's answer was that it is possible and appropriate to preserve aspects of Hegel's social philosophy as 'humanistic' bulwarks against dialectical materialism, and this diagnosis issued in a qualified call 'back to Hegel' that was crucial for Gramsci's brand of Marxism. 'Back to Hegel' is not 'back to Kant' for Croce: along with 'vulgar materialism', he rejects key aspects of Kant's transcendental philosophy.

Taking this together with its counterpart, the neo-Kantian rejection of Hegel, it might stand to reason that 'back to Kant' and 'back to Hegel' are contradictory rallying cries. But, if some advanced, recent scholarship in German philosophy of the late-eighteenth and early-nineteenth centuries is sound, perhaps they are merely contraries, which can be seen as compatible once one adjusts what 'Kant' and 'Hegel' mean in the rival formulations of their positions. For, another version of 'back to Kant' runsthroughGerman idealism, understood now as an etiolated form of Kantianism. This version, 'back to KantandHegel', is the distinctive version of the 'back to Kant' ethos mentioned at the outset. Work in this vein typically features a Kantian understanding of Hegel's main achievements and has focused especially on topics like the claimed centrality of apperception to accounts of subjectivity and the nature of moral freedom. The approach is both a form of neo-Kantianism and neo-Hegelianism.

Despite real differences on many key points of doctrine, all three 'back to . . .' stories require adaptation of highly committed, systematic philosophical resources to environments that might be thought inimical to their health. They are, all three -- 'back to Kant', 'back to Hegel', and 'back to Hegel as Kant' -- hybrid hothouse flowers. If they flourish at all, they do so only under very precise, controlled conditions, ones that may strain philosophical credulity. One might do better, therefore, to pitch one's tent in none of the three camps. Naturally, one might doubt any one of the many 'back to . . .' stories on all sorts of grounds. Indeed, there are resources for doing so present in German philosophy of the period, and Michael Forster's two interconnected books,After Herder: Philosophy of Language in the German Tradition[=AH] andGerman Philosophy of Language: From Schlegel to Hegel and Beyond[=GPL], are vigorous and innovative invitations to look at matters quite differently. The central figure in Forster's account is Herder; indeed, the two books offer the most philosophically sustained, searching, and convincing account of Herder's philosophical achievement to date. Back to . . . Herder!?[2]
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

Promoting Herder to the first rank of classical German philosophy is not an entirely new endeavor; one can trace a powerful lineage of it in the teacher-student line of Isaiah Berlin-Charles Taylor-Frederick Beiser. What is distinctive about Forster's treatment is his insistence on the superiority of Herder to other figures who precede and follow him -- Counter-Enlightenment and Romantic types -- and whom others have discussed in contrast with the development of systematic idealism, e.g., Hamann, Friedrich Schlegel, Schleiermacher, and Wilhelm von Humboldt. The basis for the claimed superiority is the relation of Herder's philosophy of language to his philosophy of mind and to his social philosophy.

As Forster sets the stage, French and British philosophy of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries did not treat language as its main focus, although philosophy of that period did have plenty to say about the nature of language. To put matters broadly, modern philosophy in large part approached language on the model of a code for thought: thought and language are primarily distinct phenomena, language playing the role of vehicle for the transmission of thought, which was considered to be complete and well-formed quite apart from language. This 'thought first' view arose for a complex variety of historical reasons that are themselves highly interesting to ponder, and there were notable partial exceptions to the view present in the modern period. But Forster allows an idealized 'thought first' view to play the role of a backdrop to his main concern, the decisive step in German philosophy away from the idea of language as mere cognitive intermediary. Such idealizations are entirely appropriate; one wouldn't know where to begin without them.

When one turns to a consideration of the 'language first' strain in German philosophy of this period, one immediately faces knotty questions of historical precedence, concerning who was the key revolutionary in the movement. Berlin typically assigns precedence to Hamann over Herder and tends to assimilate many of the latter's views on the thought-language relation to those of the former.[3]This interpretative stratagem allows Berlin to construe Herder in turn as an 'irrationalist' and to draw further negative consequences from that assignment. Forster dissents strongly from this view, and one can see why. Putting Herder in the train of Hamann allows Berlin to downplay the progressive naturalism of Herder's position, and Forster wants none of that downplaying. To this end Forster offers evidence that the idea of linguistic and cognitive co-dependency was already present in Herder's writings prior to substantial influence by Hamann. The evidence seems to me equivocal. Perhaps the best that can be done given the current state of historical knowledge -- as Forster ultimately allows -- is to mark that making more radical the language-thought relationship was in the air and venture that Hamann and Herder developed interlinking accounts from a common base.[4]

What is at issue here, however, is much more than historical house cleaning. There are important conceptual stakes, two of which stand out particularly, concerning: (1) the nature of the relative empirical commitments of Hamann and Herder and (2) the strength of their rival views of the relation of thought to language. (2) is philosophically more important, but (1) deserves brief comment.},
}

@book{berlin2000three,
  author = {Berlin, Isaiah},
  editor = {Hardy, Henry},
  title = {Three Critics of the Enlightenment: Vico, Hamann, Herder},
  year = {2000},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  address = {Princeton},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: An expanded and revised version of Berlin's Herder study, consolidating his claim that Herder inaugurated the counter-Enlightenment and that his pluralism—the doctrine that each culture has its own "center of gravity" and that cultures are incommensurable—constitutes a decisive philosophical break with Enlightenment universalism.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most widely cited statement of Berlin's reading and the primary target for critics who dispute his attribution of pluralism versus relativism and his narrative of the Counter-Enlightenment; central to reconstructing the Berlin-Herder debate.

  POSITION: Berlin's pluralist/counter-Enlightenment reading of Herder.
  },
  keywords = {value-pluralism, Berlin, Counter-Enlightenment, Herder-interpretation, High},
  abstract = {For its essay competition of 1771, the Berlin Academy of Sciences sought responses to the questions "Supposing men abandoned to their natural faculties, are they in a position to invent language? And by what means will they arrive at this invention?" As is well known, the winning essay was J. G. Herder's "On the Origin of Language." Less well known, however, is that this competition was actually the second the Academy  devoted to the topic of language. The first contest, held in 1759, was on the question: "What is the reciprocal influence of the opinions of people on language, and of language on opinions?" The prize in that competition was awarded to the orientalist Johann David Michaelis. It is the first contest that provides the main topic for Avi Lifschitz's lucid and engaging book, as he considers the historical, philosophical, and political circumstances that led to its proposal and the broader scholarly views of the now little-known (among historians of philosophy) prize-winner.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

The initial chapters inLanguage and Enlightenmentare devoted to disentangling two distinct but related questions concerning language that were widely debated in the 18thcentury. The first, "diachronic" question has to do with the issue of the origin of language, and in particular whether the emergence of language can be accounted for naturalistically (in a manner inspired by Epicurean theory) or whether divine agency must at some point be invoked. The second, "synchronic" question concerns "the reciprocal relationship between linguistic signs and human cognition" (p. 12), that is, how language and thought mutually influence one another. While these questions are conceptually distinct, Lifschitz stresses that they were not neatly separated by thinkers in this period but rather formed two sides of a single theoretical investigation into language (such that, for instance, many submissions to the 1759 competition focused on the diachronic question to the exclusion of the synchronic one that is its express topic).

With respect to these issues, Lifschitz briefly surveys the well-known discussions on the part of major early Modern thinkers, including Hobbes, Locke, Condillac, and Rousseau. In addition, Lifschitz considers the German contributions on these topics and documents what he characterizes as a "wide-ranging focus on language" (p. 64), and particularly a focus on the synchronic relation between signs and thoughts that he takes to be evident in German philosophy, aesthetics, history, and theology in the first half of the 18thcentury. Lifschitz's presentation of the views of Leibniz, Wolff, the Baumgartens, among other (even) more minor figures on this score is certainly illuminating, yet I suspect that most would be struck by how little sustained and sophisticated philosophical treatment is actually devoted to language among German thinkers in this period (a legacy apparently inherited by Kant), at least in comparison with their more prestigious contemporaries. Wolff, for instance, allows for a close connection between reason and the use of language (the former is a condition of the latter),[1]follows Locke by including a chapter on signs in hisGerman Logic, yet shows little interest in the philosophical issues that occupied and would occupy English and French thinkers, as his real focus remains on the ideas and mental acts that underlie and give rise to words and propositions. Rather than cultivating a philosophical interest in language, it seems that these early German thinkers took a more straightforwardly cultural interest in promoting the German language, a goal that perhaps presumes a sort of interrelation of language and opinion but at any rate did not lead them to subject this interrelation to any serious scrutiny.

Part, I think, of what leads Lifschitz to overstate the importance of language for early German thinkers is the close connection he asserts between clear but indistinct ideas and symbolic cognition (cf. p. 41, where he refers to the latter as a "related epistemic act"), in accordance with which he considers the rehabilitation of the confused ideas (most famously in Alexander Baumgarten'sAesthetica) as evidence of a growing interest in language. That such a connection obtains is, however, far from clear: in his best-known account, Leibniz characterizes symbolic cognition as a way in which adistinctidea is known adequately, but where signs (including words) are used to represent parts of that concept without grasping their precise meaning (and so they areat that timegrasped only obscurely).[2]This is only to say, then, that during the course of a "lengthy analysis" of a complex concept it may be necessary (due to our cognitive limitations) to deploy a sign (whether linguistic or mathematical) without focusing on its meaning, and not, as Lifschitz claims, that "in their perception ofmostideas human beings [make] use of blind or symbolic cognition" (p. 43, my emphasis).

While one might thus quibble with Lifschitz's attempt to find deep roots in the Leibnizian-Wolffian philosophy for the 1759 Academy question, there is no doubting that in the distinctivemilieuin Berlin of the 1750s a number of thinkers took an active interest in language, its role in framing social institutions, and its relation to the mind, primarily under the influence of the work of Condillac and Rousseau. These include the president of the Academy, Pierre Louis Moreau de Maupertuis (who wrote a number of papers on language and its origin), Moses Mendelssohn (who translated Rousseau'sDiscours sur l'inégalitéinto German, with an accompanying critical essay, in 1756), and Johann Peter Süssmilch (whose rebuttals of Maupertuis were delivered in 1756 but published in 1766). Alongside these discussions of the origin of language, Lifschitz shows that there was lively internal discussion among Academy members regarding the (synchronic) connection between language and opinions, particularly regarding the contentious choice of French as the language of the Academy.

This is all to say that already in the 1750s and well before the Academy competition of 1771, mainstream Enlightenment figures recognized the "linguistic rootedness of all human forms of life" and the importance of language as a "tool of cognition" (p. 6). That this is so, Lifschitz rightly contends, constitutes a significant challenge to the view that such an interest in language, with its accompanying focus on the historical and non-rational aspects of human nature, was proper to the figures of the counter-Enlightenment, such as Herder and Hamann. As Lifschitz contends, this amounts to a direct challenge to the characterization of the counter-Enlightenment presented in Isaiah Berlin's seminal studies. It also challenges more recent contributions, such as Michael Forster's work on Herder's philosophy of language. Lifschitz can be taken to show that Herder's claim, as characterized by Forster, that "thought is essentially dependent upon and bounded by language" and that "one cannot think unless one has a language and one can only think what one can express linguistically"[3]must be taken in the broader context of these earlier philosophical (and political) debates. For these reasons, Lifschitz's deft treatment of the complex debates taking place in Berlin in the 1750s is, I think, the most rewarding part of this book.

These various currents of discussion are shown to come together in Michaelis' prize-winning essay (published in 1759 in French translation asDissertation . . . sur l'influence réciproque du langage sur les opinions, et des opinions sur le langage). TheDissertationis anything but the dry contribution of a biblical scholar. Michaelis answers the Academy's question by attacking the linguistic barbarisms of scholars and scientists and championing the use of vernacular, in contrast to efforts on the part of theorists since Leibniz to craft a universal language. Notably, Michaelis offers an account of the development of language that is attentive to the key role played by historical accident and even compares the authority of the individual with respect to influencing the growth of language to that of a citizen in a democratic state. With respect to the specific issue of the mutual influence of language and opinions, Michaelis offers a view of language as a sort of archive that encodes the history of a nation's opinions (both true and false), which are accessible through the use of etymology. This account emphasizes the contingency of the development of language as well as its indispensability for thought. In light of this, it is unsurprising that Michaelis' essay served as a catalyst for wider discussion and more intense interest in language among a number of major German thinkers. These included, as Lifschitz documents, Mendelssohn (who praised Michaelis but criticized the ambiguities of the question), Hamann (who wrote two critiques of the essay), and Herder (who in spite of criticism of Michaelis seems to have adopted a number of his positions (p. 175)).

All told, Lifschitz offers an eye-opening account of an episode in the history of German thought, which, though largely overlooked, clearly holds significant philosophical promise. While a philosophical audience will likely find it longer on historical detail and shorter on argumentative reconstruction than they are perhaps used to, it nonetheless serves to mark out some very promising territory for further investigation, and it is to be hoped that scholars interested in the philosophy of language and its history will take an interest in following up on this lively and provocative discussion.},
}

@article{sikka2005enlightened,
  author = {Sikka, Sonia},
  title = {Enlightened relativism},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {2005},
  volume = {31},
  number = {3},
  pages = {309--341},
  doi = {10.1177/0191453705051708},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Sikka argues that Herder's position combines a presumption of basic human goods with contextual interpretation of practices and values, constituting an "enlightened" relativism that acknowledges universal criteria while denying that any single cultural model can be imposed as the standard for all.

  RELEVANCE: This article develops in compressed form the thesis later expanded in the 2011 book; useful for understanding the philosophical argument about how Herder's universalism and relativism are reconciled, specifically bearing on Humanitat as a cross-cultural standard.

  POSITION: Enlightened/qualified relativism, against both pure relativism and Enlightenment universalism.
  },
  keywords = {cultural-relativism, Humanitat, enlightened-relativism, philosophy-social-criticism, Medium},
  abstract = {Johann Gottfried Herder has been described as the founder of cultural relativism within the German philosophical tradition, which would make him the starting-point for one thread in the pattern of ideas leading to the Nazi disaster. More recently, some scholars have rejected this interpretation, arguing that Herder actually supported the universalist values of the Enlightenment. I argue that Herder’s position is actually a complex, and laudable, blend of universalism and relativism. It includes: (1) the presumption of a set of basic human goods, upon which some universal criteria for ethical judgements may be founded: and, (2) the view that human practices, values and beliefs must be interpreted within their social context, and that the happiness and virtue of individuals can only be measured in relation to their specific values, being a function of their capacity to satisfy their desires and to live up to their ideals.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{forster2018herder,
  author = {Forster, Michael N.},
  title = {Herder's Philosophy},
  year = {2018},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  address = {Oxford},
  doi = {10.1093/oso/9780199588367.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Forster's comprehensive systematic study treats Herder as a sophisticated philosopher whose positions on language, mind, history, and ethics are internally coherent. On the relativism question, Forster acknowledges Herder's relativistic impulses while arguing they are partially restrained by his anthropological universalism, though tensions remain unresolved.

  RELEVANCE: The most authoritative contemporary philosophical treatment of Herder; provides the systematic context within which the relativism/pluralism question must be situated, and addresses whether Herder's position is philosophically defensible in its own terms.

  POSITION: Philosophical reconstruction of Herder's full system; qualified acknowledgment of relativism with universalist constraints.
  },
  keywords = {Herder-interpretation, systematic-philosophy, relativism, universalism, High},
  abstract = {This book considers Herder’s philosophy in the round and argues that it is both far more impressive in quality and far more influential in modern thought than has previously been realized. After an introduction on Herder’s intellectual biography, philosophical style, and general program in philosophy, there are chapters on his philosophy of language, his hermeneutics, his theory of translation, his contribution of the philosophical foundations for both linguistics and cultural anthropology, his philosophy of mind, his aesthetics, his moral philosophy, his philosophy of history, his political philosophy, his philosophy of religion, and his intellectual influence. The book argues that he contributed vitally important ideas in all of these areas; that in many of them his ideas were seminal for subsequent philosophers, including Friedrich Schlegel, Schleiermacher, Wilhelm von Humboldt, Hegel, and Nietzsche; that they indeed founded whole new disciplines, such as linguistics, anthropology, and comparative literature; and that moreover they were in many cases even better than what these subsequent thinkers and disciplines went on to make of them.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{zammito2010herder,
  author = {Zammito, John H. and Menges, Karl and Menze, Ernest A.},
  title = {Johann Gottfried Herder Revisited: The Revolution in Scholarship in the Last Quarter Century},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {71},
  number = {4},
  pages = {661--684},
  doi = {10.1353/jhi.2010.0006},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This review essay surveys the dramatic revision in Herder scholarship since the 1980s: the turn away from nationalist-relativist readings toward a philosophically sophisticated Herder whose ideas on language, history, and culture are seen as continuous with, rather than opposed to, the Enlightenment. The article documents the emergence of Sikka's, Forster's, and others' revisionist accounts.

  RELEVANCE: Essential historiographical orientation for understanding the current state of the relativism/pluralism debate in Herder scholarship; establishes why Berlin's influential reading has been contested and what alternatives have emerged.

  POSITION: Scholarly survey; critical of older relativist/nationalist readings.
  },
  keywords = {Herder-scholarship, historiography, revisionism, relativism, High},
  abstract = {A veritable tidal shift in Herder scholarship has taken place over the last quarter century, primarily but not exclusively in German. This review essay seeks to evoke the richness and vitality of this revival with the hope of persuading American academics that some ill-founded opinions still circulating concerning Herder's "irrationalism" and chauvinistic, even racist nationalism, and his philosophical naivety and literary effrontery, might at last be put to rest. The recent revival has brough sharply to the fore two crucial aspects of Herder. First, there is the contribution of Herder's thought to the emergent cultural and social sciences. Second, for Herder the "science of man" was also a natural science: the division between the humanities and the natural sciences that has been such a hallmark of the age from Kant until very recently did not exist for Herder.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{spencer2007herder,
  author = {Spencer, Vicki},
  title = {In Defense of Herder on Cultural Diversity and Interaction},
  journal = {The Review of Politics},
  year = {2007},
  volume = {69},
  number = {1},
  pages = {79--105},
  doi = {10.1017/s0034670507000320},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Spencer defends Herder against the charge that his celebration of cultural particularity leads to cultural closure and relativism, arguing that Herder actually valued cultural interaction and exchange and that his pluralism entails neither relativism nor the rejection of cross-cultural judgment.

  RELEVANCE: Directly engages the key question of whether Herder's pluralism can accommodate cross-cultural evaluation, providing a defense of Herder that parallels Berlin's but grounds it more carefully in Herder's own texts on cultural exchange and Humanitat.

  POSITION: Defense of Herder's cultural pluralism against relativism; pluralism-with-universalism reading.
  },
  keywords = {cultural-diversity, cultural-interaction, pluralism, relativism, High},
  abstract = {Diversity and unity are pivotal to the thought of the eighteenth-century German philosopher, Johann Gottfried Herder. Yet despite the enormous influence of his thought on the late Isaiah Berlin in the development of his own pluralism and Berlin's urging for those interested in cultural diversity to learn from Herder, many commentators fail to heed Berlin's equal recognition of Herder's radical antidualism. Thus, while an increasing number of theorists now note Herder's contribution to philosophical and political thought in being one of the first to recognize the value of cultural diversity, many fail to grasp fully the complexity and subtlety of his understanding of culture. Contrary to what is becoming the orthodox view in Anglo-American political theory, Herder was fully aware of the diversity existing within any given cultural community and promoted cultural interaction and interchange in a spirit of cooperation. He only opposed the cultural domination of indigenous populations.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{spencer2012herder,
  author = {Spencer, Vicki A.},
  title = {Herder's Political Thought: {A} Study on Language, Culture and Community},
  year = {2012},
  publisher = {University of Toronto Press},
  doi = {10.3138/9781442695344},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Spencer's book-length study argues that Herder's political thought
  is organised around an expressivist theory of language: language is constitutive
  of both individual identity and communal culture, and political community is
  therefore legitimate only insofar as it enables the flourishing of the linguistic-
  cultural community (Volk). Spencer defends Herder against the charge of ethnic
  nationalism and argues his thought supports a multicultural liberal model of
  political community.

  RELEVANCE: The most thorough recent monograph on Herder as political thinker;
  covers language, Volk, community, and the relationship between cultural diversity
  and political order -- all central topics of this project.

  POSITION: Expressivist-communitarian reading of Herder's politics; challenges
  ethnic-nationalist interpretations.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, political-thought, language, Volk, community, High},
  abstract = {Johann Gottfried Herder was a philosopher and important intellectual presence in eighteenth-century Germany. Herder's Political Thought examines the work of this significant figure in the context of both historical and contemporary developments in political philosophy.Vicki A. Spencer reveals Herder as one of the first Western philosophers to grapple seriously with cultural diversity without abandoning a commitment to universal values and the first to make language and culture an issue of justice. As Spencer argues, both have made Herder a source of inspiration for the pluralist turn of contemporary political philosophy. Contending that in an era of globalization, it is no longer possible to ignore Herder's crucial insights on the relationship between cultural membership and individual identity, Spencer demonstrates how these ideas can help us understand, and perhaps resolve, the linguistic and cultural-political struggles of our times},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{piirimae2015berlin,
  author = {Piirim\"{a}e, Eva},
  title = {Berlin, Herder, and the Counter-Enlightenment},
  journal = {Eighteenth-Century Studies},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {49},
  number = {1},
  pages = {71--76},
  doi = {10.1353/ecs.2015.0042},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Piirimae argues that Berlin's reading of Herder as a founding figure of the Counter-Enlightenment is historiographically questionable, because it imposes a narrative structure that misrepresents Herder's complex and largely continuous relationship with Enlightenment values, distorting the picture of his pluralism and relativism.

  RELEVANCE: A critical assessment of the Berlin framework that grounds discussion of whether Herder is a pluralist or relativist; challenges the narrative structure within which Berlin's reading operates and thereby opens space for alternative interpretations.

  POSITION: Critical of Berlin's Counter-Enlightenment narrative; argues Herder's relation to the Enlightenment is more complex.
  },
  keywords = {Berlin, Counter-Enlightenment, Herder-interpretation, historiography, Medium},
  abstract = {This short essay discusses the usefulness and contemporary relevance of the notion of the Counter-Enlightenment, taking as its starting point Isaiah Berlin’s broader politico-intellectual concerns and interests in popularizing this term (the foundations of liberalism, moral relativism/pluralism, the origins and varieties of nationalism). It argues that all these issues are still relevant today, but that we are better off exploring them without this highly problematic category. Not only does it posit an illegitimate divide between French and German moral and political thought in the eighteenth century, but it also proves deeply misleading about the ideas of individual authors such as Johann Gottfried Herder.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{whitton1988herder,
  author = {Whitton, Brian J.},
  title = {Herder's Critique of the Enlightenment: Cultural Community versus Cosmopolitan Rationalism},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {1988},
  volume = {27},
  number = {2},
  pages = {146--168},
  doi = {10.2307/2505139},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Whitton argues that Herder's fundamental critique of Enlightenment rationalism rests on defending cultural community and particularity against cosmopolitan abstraction; however, Whitton reads this as yielding a form of cultural relativism rather than Berlin's value pluralism, since Herder's communitarian emphasis undermines the possibility of universal moral criteria.

  RELEVANCE: An important early scholarly articulation of the tension between Herder's culturalism and universalism; argues for the relativist rather than pluralist reading, providing a counterpoint to Berlin and a predecessor for more recent debates.

  POSITION: Relativist/communitarian reading; critical of Enlightenment universalism in Herder's name.
  },
  keywords = {Enlightenment-critique, cultural-community, cosmopolitanism, relativism, Medium},
  abstract = {Recent continental social theory has seen the emergence of a body of literature which represents a radical challenge to the primary concerns and assumptions of traditional Western social and political thought. While this challenge involves a number of aspects and embraces a heterogeneous group of thinkers, one theme common to them all is their opposition to the attempts of traditional scientific, social, and political disciplines to construct general theoretical programs as guides to the practical actualization of a rational order within human society, however defined. What is most objectionable about this general project for such thinkers, included among them figures like Foucault, Lyotard, and Derrida, is its implicit assumption of the possibility of establishing a hierarchy in the forms of knowledge an ordering of discourses in accordance with their relative approximation to the objective principles of an ultimate discourse of reason or truth. Their special sensitivity to language, and their shared belief in the infinite creative potential of human linguistic activity,1 led these thinkers to attack such rationalist discourses as legitimating forms for exclusionary practices which repress the full diversity of discursive interpretations of the real implicit in human linguistic activity in favor of one dominant, repressive discourse. Far from being unique to the postmodernist writers of the late twentieth century, however, this type of relativistic critique of totalizing discourses has some notable antecedents in modern social thought. The purpose of this article is to examine one such precursor of this form of critique presented in the writings of the eighteenthcentury theorist, Gottfried von Herder. In his theory of history Herder presents a radical critique of the rationalist discourse of cosmopolitan human development advanced by the Enlightenment thinkers of his day, one which is predicated upon a profound sensitivity to the importance of language in the process of historical human development. In the},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{yack2012significance,
  author = {Yack, Bernard},
  title = {The significance of Isaiah Berlin's Counter-Enlightenment},
  journal = {European Journal of Political Theory},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {12},
  number = {1},
  pages = {49--60},
  doi = {10.1177/1474885112463649},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Yack argues that Berlin's account of the Counter-Enlightenment misidentifies the real significance of Herder's pluralism: rather than stopping ancient monism, Herder's contribution was to rehabilitate prejudice and cultural particularity as sources of virtue against the Enlightenment's project of eliminating obstacles to knowledge. This reframing affects how Herder's relationship to relativism should be understood.

  RELEVANCE: Challenges Berlin's reading of Herder's pluralism from the direction of intellectual history, offering a different account of what Herder's critique of the Enlightenment was actually about; bears on whether Berlin's pluralist characterization accurately captures Herder's position.

  POSITION: Critical of Berlin's framing; reconstructs Herder's anti-Enlightenment position differently.
  },
  keywords = {Berlin, Counter-Enlightenment, pluralism, Herder-interpretation, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper takes a close look at Berlin’s claim that the emergence of Counter-Enlightenment pluralism marks a momentous historical watershed. It concludes that Berlin is right to draw our attention to the importance of this event, but that he seriously misinterprets its significance. He has good reason, in particular, to treat Herder as ‘the most formidable adversary of the French philosophes and their German disciples’, but not because Herder put a stop to the ancient creed of monism on which they relied. For Berlin’s monistic interpretation of the French Enlightenment, I shall show, badly misrepresents that intellectual movement and its impact on the world. The great significance of Herder’s pluralist critique of the Enlightenment lies, instead, in the way in which it rehabilitates prejudice as a source of human virtue and creativity, a critique that directly attacks the core mission of the philosophes: to remove the obstacles to the gathering, preservation and dissemination of useful knowledge.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{norton2007myth,
  author = {Norton, Robert Edward},
  title = {The Myth of the Counter-Enlightenment},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year = {2007},
  volume = {68},
  number = {4},
  pages = {635--658},
  doi = {10.1353/jhi.2007.0040},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Norton argues that the "Counter-Enlightenment" is Berlin's intellectual invention rather than a genuine historical movement: thinkers identified by Berlin as counter-Enlightenment figures, including Herder, did not self-consciously oppose the Enlightenment in the way Berlin depicts. This critique undermines the standard Berlin-derived framework for situating Herder's pluralism.

  RELEVANCE: If the Counter-Enlightenment is a myth, the narrative structure that gives Berlin's pluralist reading its force—Herder as the great opponent of Enlightenment monism—is significantly weakened, bearing directly on how Herder's pluralism and its relation to relativism should be understood.

  POSITION: Critical of Berlin's Counter-Enlightenment narrative; historical revisionism.
  },
  keywords = {Counter-Enlightenment, Berlin, historiography, Herder-interpretation, Medium},
  abstract = {Use of the word "Counter-Enlightenment" has become increasingly frequent in scholarly and journalistic writing. The word was almost certainly invented by the late Sir Isaiah Berlin, and it is owing to his enormous prestige and on-going influence that it has gained its current familiarity. In Berlin's view, two of the most important sources of the supposed Counter-Enlightenment are J. G. Hamann and J. G. Herder. But as I show, Berlin's numerous accounts of their thought are profoundly flawed and reflect not Herder's or Hamann's own thought, but rather that of late nineteenth- and early twentieth-century German historians, who wished to legitimate their own anti-democratic, and hence anti-Enlightenment, ideology through the construction of a false historical genealogy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{piirimae2023herder,
  author = {Piirim\"{a}e, Eva},
  title = {Herder and Enlightenment Politics},
  year = {2023},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  doi = {10.1017/9781009263825},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Piirimae reconstructs Herder's political thought through engagement with his Enlightenment contemporaries, arguing that Herder developed a distinctive vision of human self-determination, natural sociability, and cultural reform that was continuous with rather than opposed to Enlightenment values—challenging both the relativist and Counter-Enlightenment readings.

  RELEVANCE: The most recent monograph on Herder's politics; provides fresh contextualization of the universalism/relativism question by situating Herder's cultural pluralism within his broader political philosophy and his dialogue with Enlightenment figures.

  POSITION: Herder as Enlightenment thinker; revisionist reading against Counter-Enlightenment/relativist narrative.
  },
  keywords = {Enlightenment-politics, cultural-pluralism, self-determination, revisionism, High},
  abstract = {Johann Gottfried Herder initiated the modern disciplines of philosophical anthropology and cultural history, including the study of popular culture. He is also remembered as a sharp critic of colonialism and imperialism. But what types of social, economic and political arrangements did Herder envision for modern European societies? Herder and Enlightenment Politics provides a radically new interpretation of Herder's political thought, situating his ideas in Enlightenment debates on modern patriotism, commerce and peace. By reconstructing Herder's engagement with Rousseau, Montesquieu, Abbt, Ferguson, Möser, Kant and many other contemporary authors, Eva Piirimäe shows that Herder was deeply interested in the potential for cultural, moral and political reform in Russia, Germany and Europe. Herder probed the foundations of modern liberty, community and peace, developing a distinctive understanding of human self-determination, natural sociability and modern patriotism as well as advocating a vision of Europe as a commercially and culturally interconnected community of peoples.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{bollacher2014individualism,
  author = {Bollacher, Martin},
  title = {Individualism and Universalism in {H}erder's Conception of the Philosophy of History},
  journal = {Publications of the English Goethe Society},
  year = {2014},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Bollacher analyzes Herder's "globe ontology" as a structural device for holding together the tension between the individuality of diverse peoples and the universal identity of the human species; while the globe model rejects hierarchical theories of culture, it relies on a teleological idea of humanity that is embedded in the Western philosophical tradition.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the philosophical structure of Herder's position on universalism versus particularism, essential for evaluating whether Humanitat functions as a genuine normative standard or merely as a formal telos that does not block relativism.

  POSITION: Argues for a nuanced dual commitment—pluralism of cultures within a universal teleology—that does not resolve into either pure relativism or universalism.
  },
  keywords = {individualism, universalism, philosophy-of-history, Humanitat, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{barnard1965herder,
  author = {Barnard, F. M.},
  title = {Herder's Social and Political Thought: From Enlightenment to Nationalism},
  year = {1965},
  publisher = {Clarendon Press},
  address = {Oxford},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Barnard argues that Herder's political thought charts a course from
  Enlightenment universalism toward a culturally grounded nationalism, with the Volk
  concept serving as the mediating category: each people's language, customs, and
  traditions constitute the legitimate basis for its political life, and genuine
  humanity can only be achieved through, not despite, particular national cultures.
  Barnard insists Herder's nationalism is cultural and expressivist rather than
  ethnic or statist.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational monograph for the entire debate about Herder
  and nationalism; virtually all subsequent scholarship on Volk and nationhood
  either builds on or responds to Barnard's framework. It provides the interpretive
  scaffolding for the cosmopolitan-nationalist tension central to this project.

  POSITION: Defends the cultural-nationalist (as opposed to ethnic-nationalist)
  reading of Herder's political philosophy.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Volk, nationalism, Enlightenment, political-thought, High, INCOMPLETE},
}

@article{barnard1983national,
  author = {Barnard, F. M.},
  title = {National Culture and Political Legitimacy: {Herder} and {Rousseau}},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year = {1983},
  volume = {44},
  number = {2},
  pages = {231--253},
  doi = {10.2307/2709138},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Barnard examines how both Herder and Rousseau ground political
  legitimacy in the cultural particularity of a people, but argues that Herder's
  account differs from Rousseau's in prioritising expressive culture -- language,
  folk poetry, communal memory -- over abstract general will. For Herder, the
  nation's political legitimacy derives from its cultural distinctiveness, not from
  a contract among individuals.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the theoretical core of Barnard's interpretation: the
  link between cultural-national identity and political self-determination. Directly
  bears on what "Volk" means politically and why Herder thinks nationally bounded
  states are "natural."

  POSITION: Cultural-expressivist account of political legitimacy; bridges Herder's
  philosophy of culture and his political thought.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, Rousseau, nationalism, political-legitimacy, Volk, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{berlin2014three,
  author = {Berlin, Isaiah},
  title = {Three Critics of the {Enlightenment}: {Vico}, {Hamann}, {Herder}},
  year = {2014},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  doi = {10.1515/9781400848522},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Berlin presents Herder as one of the three great counter-
  Enlightenment thinkers, crediting him with inventing the concepts of Volksgeist,
  cultural belonging, and the incommensurability of values across historical periods
  and peoples. For Berlin, Herder is the originator of the idea that each culture
  has its own centre of gravity and that imposing universal standards is a form of
  intellectual imperialism. Berlin acknowledges Herder's pluralism is benign but
  argues it supplied the vocabulary later radicalized into aggressive nationalism.

  RELEVANCE: Berlin's influential interpretation shaped the entire anglophone
  reception of Herder on nationalism; his framing of Herder as "father of
  nationalism" is the foil against which Barnard, Spencer, and Sikka all argue.
  The "misappropriation" question is partly a response to Berlin.

  POSITION: Herder as transitional figure: anti-Enlightenment pluralist whose
  concepts were seized by later, more aggressive nationalists.
  },
  keywords = {Herder, counter-Enlightenment, nationalism, Volksgeist, Berlin, High},
  abstract = {Isaiah Berlin was deeply admired during his life, but his full contribution was perhaps underestimated because of his preference for the long essay form. The efforts of Henry Hardy to edit Berlin's work and reintroduce it to a broad, eager readership have gone far to remedy this. Now, Princeton is pleased to return to print, under one cover, Berlin's essays on these celebrated and captivating intellectual portraits: Vico, Hamann, and Herder. These essays on three relatively uncelebrated thinkers are not marginal ruminations, but rather among Berlin's most important studies in the history of ideas. They are integral to his central project: the critical recovery of the ideas of the Counter-Enlightenment and the explanation of its appeal and consequences--both positive and (often) tragic. Giambattista Vico was the anachronistic and impoverished Neapolitan philosopher sometimes credited with founding the human sciences. He opposed Enlightenment methods as cold and fallacious. J. G. Hamann was a pious, cranky dilettante in a peripheral German city. But he was brilliant enough to gain the audience of Kant, Goethe, and Moses Mendelssohn. In Hamann's chaotic and long-ignored writings, Berlin finds the first strong attack on Enlightenment rationalism and a wholly original source of the coming swell of romanticism. Johann Gottfried Herder, the progenitor of populism and European nationalism, rejected universalism and rationalism but championed cultural pluralism. Individually, these fascinating intellectual biographies reveal Berlin's own great intelligence, learning, and generosity, as well as the passionate genius of his subjects. Together, they constitute an arresting interpretation of romanticism's precursors. In Hamann's railings and the more considered writings of Vico and Herder, Berlin finds critics of the Enlightenment worthy of our careful attention. But he identifies much that is misguided in their rejection of universal values, rationalism, and science. With his customary emphasis on the frightening power of ideas, Berlin traces much of the next centuries' irrationalism and suffering to the historicism and particularism they advocated. What Berlin has to say about these long-dead thinkers--in appreciation and dissent--is remarkably timely in a day when Enlightenment beliefs are being challenged not just by academics but by politicians and by powerful nationalist and fundamentalist movements. The study of J. G. Hamann was originally published under the title The Magus of the North: J. G. Hamann and the Origins of Modern Irrationalism . The essays on Vico and Herder were originally published as Vico and Herder: Two Studies in the History of Ideas . Both are out of print. This new edition includes a number of previously uncollected pieces on Vico and Herder, two interesting passages excluded from the first edition of the essay on Hamann, and Berlin's thoughtful responses to two reviewers of that same edition.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{patten2010most,
