  publisher = {Brill},
  pages = {158--182},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004426870_008},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Wildschut examines how Herder uses analogy and
  Einfühlung as complementary strategies for overcoming the
  incommensurability between historical epochs; analogy provides
  structural bridges across cultural difference while Einfühlung
  supplies the imaginative-affective dimension needed to grasp an
  epoch's inner self-understanding. The chapter argues that these two
  strategies are not in tension but together constitute Herder's
  methodologically distinctive approach to historical understanding.

  RELEVANCE: This is one of the most focused recent analyses of
  Herder's actual methodology of historical understanding, directly
  addressing the question of what Einfühlung does as an epistemic
  tool and how it handles the incommensurability that follows from
  Herder's claim that each epoch carries its standard within itself.

  POSITION: Analytical reconstruction of Herder's methodology of
  historical understanding, sympathetic to Forster's framework but
  more focused on specific epistemic mechanisms.
  },
  keywords = {Einfühlung, historical-understanding, incommensurability, High},
  abstract = {This chapter considers the function of analogical understanding in Herder’s early psychology and philosophy of history. First, it explains how Herder understood analogy to be an integral aspect of human cognition and sympathy. Second, it discusses how analogical understanding is applied in historical enquiry, by looking at both the theory and praxis of Herder’s historiography. In this way, it determines both the scope and correct use of analogies in Herder’s historical writings. Furthermore, the paper sheds light on how Herder was able to account for the workings of prejudice and encounter the ethnocentric tendencies of Enlightenment philosophy of history. As a starting-point of historical understanding, analogical understanding has the function of opening up to enquiry a plurality of possible sets of analogies. By relying on experiences they are already familiar with, human beings are able to feel their way into different ways of life. Herder thus stresses the human ability to recognise oneself in different shapes. Furthermore, Herder states that human beings can project themselves into different human forms because of the malleability of human nature. As a consequence, the role of incommensurability in Herder’s philosophy is confined to providing a critical tool for delegitimising ethnocentric interpretations of historical and cultural others.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{reichert2023herder,
  author = {Reichert, Roey},
  title = {Herder and the Limits of Einf{\"u}hlung},
  journal = {International Journal of Philosophical Studies},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {31},
  number = {2},
  pages = {232--241},
  doi = {10.1080/09672559.2023.2250182},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reichert argues that Waldow's reconstruction of
  Herder's Einfühlung as "affective immersion" is broadly correct
  and aligns Herder with the Enlightenment rather than
  counter-Enlightenment tradition, but identifies three limits that
  Waldow does not address: (1) unlimited Einfühlung risks relativism
  and incoherence; (2) it can be reductive, eliminating rather than
  preserving difference; and (3) it is speculative and lacks an
  external truth criterion. The paper thus situates Herder's method
  within a critical epistemological debate rather than as a
  straightforward alternative to Enlightenment abstraction.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the epistemological status of
  Einfühlung and the risks it runs as a method of historical
  understanding — the precise questions that any treatment of Herder's
  hermeneutics must confront. The critique is relevant to assessing
  whether Herder's claim that each epoch carries its standard within
  itself can be maintained without relativistic collapse.

  POSITION: Critical-epistemological position: Einfühlung has
  methodological virtues but also unremarked limits that need to be
  addressed.
  },
  keywords = {Einfühlung, epistemology, relativism, High},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT The fifth chapter of Experience Embodied is devoted to Herder’s theory of cognition and the epistemic merits of the capacity for ‘sympathy’, or ‘empathy’ – what Herder calls Einfühlung, and which Waldow renders more accurately as ‘affective immersion’. I situate Waldow’s reading of Herder as a member of the epistemological tradition within the debate on Herder’s relationship to the Enlightenment. Waldow’s reading, I contend, is congruent with the view of Herder as an Enlightenment, rather than anti-Enlightenment, figure. I focus on what Waldow calls ‘the problem of the conceivability of difference’ (Waldow 2020, 185) and how she charts Herder’s proposed method of Einfühlung and the need for ‘affective immersion’ to address this problem. However, I also identify three potential problems, which Waldow does not address, that can arise when Einfühlung is taken too far: the first is that it may lead to relativism, and thus to incoherence; the second is reductionism, which can eliminate, rather than draw attention to, difference – thereby achieving the opposite goal; while the third is that relying solely on Einfühlung as a method can lead us into error, as it is speculative and lacks an external truth criterion.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@incollection{piirimaeeintro2020,
  author = {Piirimäe, Eva},
  title = {Introduction: Herder's Concepts of Empathy and Sympathy},
  booktitle = {Herder on Empathy and Sympathy},
  year = {2020},
  publisher = {Brill},
  pages = {1--33},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004426870_002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Piirimäe's introduction to the collected volume
  demonstrates that Einfühlung and Sympathie are not marginal
  rhetorical devices in Herder but permeate his entire philosophy,
  from aesthetics and poetics through historiography to his account
  of moral education. She distinguishes Herder's use of these
  concepts from later (especially phenomenological) uses and situates
  them within the tradition of mid-eighteenth-century moral philosophy
  (Hutcheson, Hume, Smith) as transformed by Herder's expressivist
  philosophy of language.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the conceptual map for understanding Einfühlung
  across Herder's oeuvre, which is necessary for assessing how it
  functions in his hermeneutics and philosophy of history specifically.
  The volume it introduces is the major recent collection on this topic.

  POSITION: Comprehensive survey of Einfühlung and Sympathie as
  pervasive categories across Herder's philosophy.
  },
  keywords = {Einfühlung, sympathy, moral-philosophy, High},
  abstract = {The Introduction outlines the central themes of the volume and discusses some of the different perspectives from which Herder’s ideas about empathy and sympathy can be approached. It sketches the historical provenance and limitations of viewing his ideas through the analytical lens of German historicism and seeks to embed the findings of the current volume in contemporary Herder scholarship. It also discusses, and invites further research about, Herder’s relationship to the ancient Stoic and early modern ideas about universal sympathy, oikeiôsis, and natural sociability. Finally, the Introduction provides an overview of the individual contributions and concludes by highlighting the contemporary relevance of Herder’s thinking about empathy and sympathy.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{solanki2022listening,
  author = {Solanki, Tanvi},
  title = {Listening to Difference: J.G. Herder's Aural Theory of Cultural Diversity in the Treatise on the Origin of Language (1772)},
  journal = {History of European Ideas},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {48},
  number = {7},
  pages = {930--947},
  doi = {10.1080/01916599.2022.2064603},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Solanki argues that Herder's Treatise on the Origin
  of Language develops an "aural theory of cultural diversity" — a
  model in which listening attentively to the sounds and phonological
  structures of diverse languages discloses genuine cultural
  difference that Eurocentric visual and scriptural paradigms
  systematically suppress. The article uses Herder's critical readings
  of colonial travelogues to show that his philosophy of language is
  grounded in concrete attention to embodied, situated listening
  practices rather than abstract linguistic universalism.

  RELEVANCE: This article offers an unusual but productive reading of
  the Treatise, connecting Herder's philosophy of language directly
  to the methodological problem of understanding cultural difference
  — the same problem that his Einfühlung addresses in the context of
  historical understanding. It also shows the Treatise's engagement
  with colonial encounter, broadening the context for interpreting
  Herder's cultural particularism.

  POSITION: Postcolonial-inflected reading of Herder's language
  philosophy as grounded in embodied, attentive listening to cultural
  difference.
  },
  keywords = {philosophy-of-language, cultural-diversity, Treatise-on-Language, Medium},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT In this article, I develop the concept and practice of ‘listening to difference,’ examining J.G. Herder’s aural theory of cultural diversity as primarily worked out in the ‘Treatise on the Origin of Language’ (1772). I examine the sources Herder critiqued to outline his aural theory of linguistic and cultural difference, which have thus far only been summarily mentioned if at all in scholarship despite the prominence of the ‘Treatise’ in intellectual history and philosophy. These sources comprise the travelogues of seventeenth- and early eighteenth-century French scientists, mathematicians, diplomats, and Jesuit missionaries who described the inadequacy of Romanized orthography to accurately transcribe the sounds and pronunciations of the indigenous peoples they encountered throughout the Americas, Thailand, and China. Herder’s critical readings of travelogues showcased the unintended failures and interruptions in European colonial practices of listening. I argue that it was the French travellers’ failure to erase difference, to transform it into identity or, in other words, into a universalist monolingualism, which produced a consciousness of cultural and linguistic difference, even if repressed. The sounds of the spoken indigenous languages exposed the limits of the paradigms of Catholic and European linguistic classification. Instead, the exemplary technologies for preserving difference in scriptural and listening practices were for Herder the Hebrew alphabet and the oral transmission of poetry in ancient and Indigenous cultures. Listening to difference, as I define it, involves an openness to listening to the voices and sounds of the othered by listeners who recognize their distinct situated, embodied positionalities. It offers alternate possibilities to conceive of human diversity as continually in formation and not as a static or progressive hierarchy.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{castro2025forgotten,
  author = {Castro, Paulo Alexandre e},
  title = {The Forgotten View of the Origin of Language: The Legacy of Herder's Philosophy},
  journal = {Conatus},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {10},
  number = {1},
  pages = {73--85},
  doi = {10.12681/cjp.37087},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Castro reconstructs Herder's four natural laws of
  language in the Treatise and argues that the Treatise anticipates
  key themes in contemporary philosophy of mind and language — in
  particular, the constitutive role of reflection (Besonnenheit) and
  the priority of audition — while remaining neglected in current
  discussions that prefer later German idealist accounts. The article
  urges a recovery of Herder's naturalistic-anthropological approach
  to the emergence of language as a distinctive philosophical
  contribution.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a recent exposition of the philosophical content
  of the Treatise on the Origin of Language, the text that grounds
  Herder's account of the dependence of thought and understanding on
  linguistic-cultural particularity; useful for the project's section
  on how the Treatise supports Herder's hermeneutical methodology.

  POSITION: Herder as anticipating contemporary naturalistic philosophy
  of mind and language; recovery of the Treatise as a neglected
  contribution.
  },
  keywords = {Treatise-on-Language, philosophy-of-mind, naturalism, Medium},
  abstract = {The question about the origin of language marked modernity with approaches that still echo in contemporary thinkers. This is the case with Herder’s Treatise on the Origin of Language. The question with which Herder opens the essay is significant and expresses well the fundamental problem that marked the philosophical intentions of the 18th century, namely: “Were human beings, left to their natural abilities, able to invent language for themselves?” Forgetting for a moment the implicit reference to God that continued to mark historically the philosophical narratives, it is important to focus the question on the appearance of language. In this sense, the philosopher’s essay is not limited to hypotheses about the emergence of language, or rather, about the founding characteristics of language, but it consolidates it in the anthropological, sociological and even biological horizon from which it allows the understanding of human nature and condition. The enunciation of the four natural laws and the narrative of justification that the philosopher elaborates on reveals a strong potential to understand the phenomenon of language and the human mind. This essay seeks, first, to explain Herder’s theses; second, based on this explanation, see in what sense his approach allows us to understand the phenomenon of the origin and formation of language; and finally, to understand the scope of his work with regard to language and mind, that is, to seek to determine its legacy in contemporary philosophy, namely with regard to the understanding of the human mind. Regarding the latter, it is important to mention two essential points to understand the importance of Herder’s thought for the understanding of the human mind: the mention of reflection as an inner thought and hearing as a fundamental characteristic for the development of language. It is all these questions that this essay seeks to address.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{forster2005schleiermacher,
  author = {Forster, Michael N.},
  title = {Schleiermacher's Hermeneutics: Some Problems and Solutions},
  journal = {The Harvard Review of Philosophy},
  year = {2005},
  volume = {13},
  number = {1},
  pages = {100--122},
  doi = {10.5840/harvardreview20051315},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Forster examines several central features of
  Schleiermacher's hermeneutics — including the hermeneutic circle,
  the psychological method, and the role of divination — and argues
  that Schleiermacher significantly advanced the theory of
  interpretation he inherited from Herder while also introducing some
  philosophical difficulties (especially around the psychological
  method's reliance on direct introspective access to an author's
  mental states) that later hermeneutic theory has struggled to resolve.

  RELEVANCE: Directly relevant to the project's treatment of Herder's
  place in the hermeneutic tradition, since it shows exactly which
  features of Herderian hermeneutics Schleiermacher preserved,
  transformed, and which problems he introduced — thus mapping the
  transition from Herder to the canonical figure most often cited as
  the "founder" of modern hermeneutics.

  POSITION: Comparative analysis: Schleiermacher as indebted to
  Herder but also as introducing problematic innovations.
  },
  keywords = {Schleiermacher, hermeneutics, historical-reception, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{stueber2012understanding,
  author = {Stueber, Karsten R.},
  title = {Understanding Versus Explanation? How to Think about the Distinction between the Human and the Natural Sciences},
  journal = {Inquiry},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {55},
  number = {1},
  pages = {17--32},
  doi = {10.1080/0020174x.2012.643621},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Stueber reconstructs the methodological debate about
  Verstehen (understanding) as the distinctive method of the human
  sciences, tracing it from Droysen and Dilthey through to contemporary
  philosophy of mind and action. He argues that the debate cannot be
  settled by appeal to empathy or simulation alone, since understanding
  other agents requires both imaginative re-enactment and the application
  of rational interpretive norms — a position that calls for a more
  nuanced account than either strict Verstehen advocates or their
  naturalist critics typically provide.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the systematic philosophical context for
  assessing Herder's Einfühlung within the broader tradition of
  debate about understanding in the human sciences; useful for the
  project's discussion of how Herder's methodology anticipates and
  differs from later Diltheyan and post-Diltheyan accounts of
  Verstehen. Helps locate Herder's contribution within the
  Erklären/Verstehen distinction.

  POSITION: Analytical philosophy of social science perspective on
  the Verstehen tradition, situating Herder upstream of Dilthey.
  },
  keywords = {Verstehen, human-sciences, Dilthey, Low},
  abstract = {Abstract This essay will argue systematically and from a historical perspective that there is something to be said for the traditional claim that the human and natural sciences are distinct epistemic practices. Yet, in light of recent developments in contemporary philosophy of science, one has to be rather careful in utilizing the distinction between understanding and explanation for this purpose. One can only recognize the epistemic distinctiveness of the human sciences by recognizing the epistemic centrality of reenactive empathy for our understanding of rational agency, that is, by emphasizing the psychological component in the concept of understanding that nineteenth-century philosophers like Droysen, in contrast to twentieth-century hermeneutic philosophers, still acknowledged. In addition, the essay will show in detail that merely pointing to the fact that narratives have a cognitive function in the domain of the human sciences, as is common among philosophers of history, does not provide us with a sufficient demarcation criterion for distinguishing between the human and natural sciences. Notes 1. For various aspects of the historical debate about the distinction between understanding and explanation see the essays in Feest (Citation2010). 2. For a succinct summary of the empiricist critique of the concept of understanding, see also Abel (Citation1948). 3. For an early exception to the general low regard of understanding in analytic philosophy of science, see Friedman (Citation1974). 4. To be fair, Mink seems at times a bit ambiguous about the claim of whether or not the configurational mode of comprehension is unique to the human sciences or whether it can also be found in some natural sciences like biology (see Mink, Citation1998, p. 130). 5. In contrast, David Velleman (Citation2003) suggests that the essence of a narrative lies in its ability to initiate and resolve structured cadences of emotions in the audience. Narrative understanding is achieved by our ability to relate the events in the story to a "familiar pattern of how things feel". I tend to agree with Carroll (Citation2007) that reference to such emotional cadences only allows us only to distinguish between interesting or boring narratives (see also Currie, 2010, Ch. 2). Nevertheless, it is an interesting question why it is that those narratives that resonate with us emotionally seem to be particularly interesting to us, why it is that we find those stories particularly important to write down or important to share with others. I suspect that narratives resonate emotionally with us for two reasons: First, it has to do with the fact that narratives invite us to take up the perspectives of the agents depicted in the narrative. Second, they resonate because they depict the actions of others in a morally salient manner activating our own moral judgment and moral emotions. Unfortunately, I do not have the space to address this important question here. It will have to wait for another occasion. 6. For a discussion of MacIntyre's view see also the insightful remarks by Uebel (this issue & forthcoming). 7. This should however not be understood as implying that I adopt a non-causal account of reasons. Au contraire, I continue to conceive of reasons as causes. See my forthcoming "Explaining human agency: Reasons, causes, and the first person perspective". 8. We thus experience a case of imaginative resistance. For a more detailed analysis of this phenomenon see Stueber (Citation2011). 9. The argument that I have outlined here does have obvious applications for a defense of simulation theory against objections by so-called narrativist positions in the theory of mind. For a brief outline of that position, see particularly Gallagher and Hutto (Citation2008). I have concentrated here on the topic of narratives as it has been discussed mainly in the philosophy of history, but not only for reasons of space. I also find that philosophers of history have been more specific about how exactly they conceive of the nature of narratives and their epistemic functions. I have directly addressed the narrativist challenge in contemporary theory of mind in Stueber, in press a & b. 10. Among twentieth-century philosophers of history, R. G. Collingwood and his follower William Dray constitute the exception in this respect. For my interpretation of Collingwood see Stueber (Citation2002). 11. This article has benefitted from discussions at the conference on Current Topics in the Philosophy of Human Sciences (TU Berlin, 2010), the meeting of the Society for the Philosophy of History at the APA Pacific Division conference (San Francisco, 2010) and the conference of the MacIntyre Society (Providence College, 2011). I also would like to thank Uljana Feest for her helpful comments on the penultimate draft.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{gjesdal2006hegel,
  author = {Gjesdal, Kristin},
  title = {Hegel and Herder on Art, History, and Reason},
  journal = {Philosophy and Literature},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {30},
  number = {1},
  pages = {17--32},
  doi = {10.1353/phl.2006.0010},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Contrasts Herder's non-essentialist, historically
  pluralistic approach to art and reason with Hegel's essentialist
  historicism. Gjesdal shows that Herder developed a philosophically
  coherent account of the historicity of art sixty years before Hegel's
  Berlin lectures, but without Hegel's teleological framework in which
  art's historical development culminates in its own supersession. Herder's
  alternative resists the "end of art" thesis that follows from Hegel's
  essentialism.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the Herder-Hegel relationship as a key
  debate in Herder's reception; shows how the systematic development of
  Herderian historical themes in Hegel introduced a teleological distortion
  absent in Herder's own account.

  POSITION: Sides with Herder's pluralist historicism over Hegel's
  essentialist version, contributing to recent rehabilitation of Herder
  against Hegelian overshadowing.
  },
  keywords = {herder-hegel, historicism, philosophy-of-history, art-history, High},
  abstract = {Hegel and Herder on Art, History, and Reason Kristin Gjesdal The introduction of a historical perspective in aesthetics is usually traced back to Hegel's 1820 lectures on fine art. Given at the University of Berlin, these lectures were amongst Hegel's most successful and best attended.1 By then a recognized intellectual figure, Hegel sets out to salvage art from its subjectivization in Kantian and romantic aesthetics, but ends up declaring that art, considered in its highest vocation, is a thing of the past. This judgment on art—that its greatness is a thing of the past—follows from Hegel's attempt to combine a notion of art's historicity with a conception of its absolute essence. Hegel, however, was not the first to think systematically about the historicity of art. For this aspect of art—and, indeed, of reason too—was explored philosophically a good sixty years before Hegel's Berlin lectures by a young and aspiring student of Kant's, namely Johann Gottfried Herder. In a series of essays and fragments, Herder develops an advanced and elaborate alternative to Hegel's conception, a fundamentally non-essentialist approach to art, history, and reason. Over the past twenty years or so, Hegel's attempt to combine an essentialist and an historicist approach to art has been revitalized, with increasing force and sophistication, by Arthur Danto.2 The attempts to rehabilitate Herder's aesthetics, however, have been few and far between.3 This is the aim of the present essay, which seeks to contrast the young Herder's non-essentialist understanding of the historicity of art with Hegel's essentialist views on the artwork's historicity, pleading for a rehabilitation of the former.4 I approach this task from three different directions. First, I offer a brief account of Herder's and Hegel's views on reason, history, and art. Second, I look closer at their conceptions of classical tragedy and sculpture, which, due to Winckelmann's studies, [End Page 17] were perceived at the time as the gateway to a historically sensitive conception of culture as such. Finally, I round off by looking at some of the hermeneutic implications of Herder's non-essentialist standpoint. I According to Hegel, a phenomenologically mature and accountable philosophy is distinguished by its readiness to reflect on its own, historical presuppositions. It must acknowledge the critical commitments of modern thinking, but also take into account how these commitments are the result of spirit's laborious journey through history. Only by combining the perspective of modernity with the resources of tradition may philosophy inhabit a place where systematicity and historicity concur. Reality may now be exhausted in the form of a speculative logic. This, according to Hegel, is the end of history, but as the end of history, it is also the dawn of a new era, that of free and self-determining spirit. Hegel's notion of the end of history testifies to a formidable optimism about the power of critical thought. In modernity, Hegel claims, art and religion no longer emerge as adequate media of self-reflection. It is philosophy that has taken on this task. Considered from the standpoint of modernity, religion and art are no longer appropriate forms of expression; now only the conceptual optics of philosophy adequately reflects our deepest truths and values.5 Artistically speaking, this moment may well emerge as a loss. For even though Hegel never claims that art, as such, will no longer be produced,6 he reckons it will not be produced in the same spirit as before. No longer expressive of a shared, ethical and political commitment, art is not brought forth by necessity. Intellectually speaking, however, the end of art triggers a different response. Not only does the end of art represent another step towards the conceptual self-determination of spirit. It also follows that only when a phenomenon such as art has finally fulfilled its historical possibilities can it be brought to philosophical clarity about itself. Only after art can we know conceptually what art is. Aesthetics can only begin at the point when art, considered in its highest vocation, no longer holds a historical future. II As a young and...},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{denby2005culture,
  author = {Denby, David},
  title = {Herder: Culture, Anthropology and the {Enlightenment}},
  journal = {History of the Human Sciences},
  year = {2005},
  volume = {18},
  number = {1},
  pages = {55--76},
  doi = {10.1177/0952695105051126},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Herder's philosophy of culture -- his insistence
  on cultural particularity, the organic unity of cultures, and the
  incommensurability of cultural values -- constitutes the philosophical
  foundation on which modern cultural anthropology was built. Denby traces
  how Herder's opposition to Enlightenment universalism provided the
  conceptual vocabulary that Boas and his school institutionalized as
  cultural relativism and participant-observation methodology.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most focused account of the Herder-to-anthropology
  transmission in the secondary literature, directly addressing the question
  of how Herderian ideas became the basis for Boasian cultural anthropology.

  POSITION: Represents the view that cultural anthropology's foundational
  methodology is Herderian in origin, not merely in spirit but in specific
  conceptual borrowings mediated through German Romanticism.
  },
  keywords = {herder-boas, cultural-anthropology, cultural-relativism, enlightenment, High},
  abstract = {The anthropological sensibility has often been seen as growing out of opposition to Enlightenment universalism. Johann Gottfried Herder (1744-1803) is often cited as an ancestor of modern cultural relativism, in which cultures exist in the plural. This article argues that Herder’s anthropology, and anthropology generally, are more closely related to Enlightenment thought than is generally considered. Herder certainly attacks Enlightenment abstraction, the arrogance of its Eurocentric historical teleology, and argues the case for a proto-hermeneutical approach which emphasizes embeddedness, horizon, the usefulness of prejudice. His suspicion of the ideology of progress and of associated theories of stadial development leads to a critique of cosmopolitanism and, particularly, of colonialism. But a comparison with a central Enlightenment figure like the natural historian Buffon (1708-88) reveals shared anthropological assumptions: human beings are characterized by the flexibility of their relationship to their environment, and by their ability to transmit and receive social knowledge. Herder’s critique of progress is thus an unstable one: culture [ Kultur ] as the process through which humanity develops can also be called Enlightenment [ Aufklärung ]. Herder’s definition of culture is much closer to a unitary Enlightenment model of civilization than is frequently suggested. Herder’s relativism is thus open to question: he holds on to certain universal criteria for transcultural judgements. This reassessment of Herder’s place in Enlightenment anthropology raises questions of contemporary relevance regarding cultural relativism on the one hand, and modernization and globalization on the other.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{makkreel1969dilthey,
  author = {Makkreel, Rudolf A.},
  title = {Wilhelm Dilthey and the {Neo-Kantians}: The Distinction of the {Geisteswissenschaften} and the {Kulturwissenschaften}},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Philosophy},
  year = {1969},
  volume = {7},
  number = {4},
  pages = {423--440},
  doi = {10.1353/hph.2008.1727},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Analyzes Dilthey's project of grounding the Geisteswissenschaften
  in relation to the rival Neo-Kantian program (Windelband, Rickert) for
  distinguishing the cultural sciences. Makkreel shows that Dilthey's
  approach, rooted in the lived experience of historical subjects and the
  hermeneutic interpretation of expressions, is fundamentally different from
  the Neo-Kantian value-theoretic approach, with the Herderian inheritance
  visible in Dilthey's insistence on historical individuality and the
  irreducibility of human expression to natural-scientific categories.

  RELEVANCE: Situates Dilthey as the crucial mediator between Herderian
  historicism and twentieth-century hermeneutics and philosophy of social
  science, clarifying how Herderian themes were transformed in the late
  nineteenth-century methodological debates.

  POSITION: Favors Dilthey's hermeneutic over the Neo-Kantian
  value-theoretic approach as the more philosophically adequate account of
  the distinctiveness of the human sciences.
  },
  keywords = {dilthey, geisteswissenschaften, neo-kantianism, hermeneutics, Medium},
  abstract = {Wilhelm Dilthey and the Neo-Kantians: The Distinction of the Geisteswissenschaften and the Kulturwissenschaften RUDOLF A. MAKKREEL WmI-mLMDILTHEY'S conception of the Geisteswissenschaften ("human studies") encompasses what we would now call the humanities and the social sciences. He formulated his concept of the Geisteswissenschaften mainly with the theoretical problems of the historian in mind. Dilthey was himself a working historian who felt that German historicists like Ranke had made great strides in freeing history from metaphysical speculation,x But Dilthey also realized that unless historicism were to obtain a new philosophical grounding, it would be exposed to the dangers of relativism. Although he considered the effort to relate historical events to their national context commendable, he looked with some distaste on the narrow political concerns of the Prussian historians who felt it their main task to justify a strong constitutional Prussian monarchy. Dilthey found Burckhardt's emphasis on culture a healthy antidote, yet criticized his method for being too vague. Dilthey was concerned about the inability of historians to utilize the results of the social sciences of their day and pointed out the need for new, more germane categories. That among these a reflective concept of experienced time should be central relates Dilthey's theory of the Geisteswissenschaften to the work on time by Bergson and the phenomenologists. Heidegger's hermeneutics of the historicity of human existence is openly indebted to Dilthey. In this essay I will attempt to delineate Dilthey's theory of the Geistes. wissenschaften by contrasting it to the theory of the Kulturwissenscha]ten ("cultural sciences") which the Neo-Kantians offered as an alternative. It should become clear that the difference between the two alternatives is not just terminological but conceptual. The Neo-Kantians basically approved of the kind of history that Burckhardt wrote and were content with the current social sciences. They Dilthey is oftenlabeled a historicist.Historicismas used here has nothing in common with Karl Popper's polemicaluse of the term. Cf. his The Poverty of Historicism (London: Routledge and Kegan Paul, 1960). Dilthey, for instance, does not establish any historical tendencies in a lawlike fashion. [423] 424 HISTORY OF PHILOSOPHY only wanted to arrive at a better conceptual analysis of them by means of their concept of culture. They rejected Dilthey's concept of the Geisteswissenscha]ten as based on a mere distinction of subject matter, thereby ignoring the fundamental methodological innovations of Dilthey's work. The word Geisteswissenschalten does not have a satisfactory English equiva. lent. Most translators have settled for the ambiguous expression "human studies." It is ironic that the German word probably originated as the translation of J. S. Mill's term "moral sciences." Erich Rothacker writes: "Perhaps the term Geisteswissenschaften first appeared in 1849 in Schiel's translation of Mill's System of Logic.'" 2 This claim is doubly surprising. Not only is the concept of the moral sciences inadequate to characterize Geisteswissenscha#en as it has come to be used, but also one would have expected earlier instances of the word, given its common usage today and the long history of th~ words Geist and Wissenschaft in the German philosophical tradition. Rothacker does cite a few isolated cases before 1849 when contemporaries or followers of Hegel employed the related expressions Wissen. schaft des Geistes and Geistwissenschaft. However, it is significant that these are in the singular. These terms can be rendered as "science of spirit" in the metaphysical sense. Karl Chr. Ft. Kranse, for example, used "science of reason" as a synonym) Geistwissenschaft means the philosophy of spirit. There can thus be only one philosophical science of spirit. Dilthey's Einleitung in die Geisteswissenschaften of 1883 is considered to have provided the classical formulation of the concept Geisteswissenschalten--a formnlation which is radically pluralistic. In this work Dilthey reveals his break with the idealistic concept of science by the attention he devotes to the rigors of empiricism. Discontented with the way that metaphysical systems distort and ignore facts, Dilthey stresses that science must not sacrifice its responsibility to be empirical in its haste to create a unified order. In France, Comte had already denounced the idea of one grand science. In its stead he established a hierarchical system...},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{hamid2023dilthey,
  author = {Hamid, Nabeel},
  title = {Anthropology and History in the Early {Dilthey}},
  journal = {Studies in History and Philosophy of Science},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {100},
  pages = {90--98},
  doi = {10.1016/j.shpsa.2023.06.002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Dilthey's reception of the new empirical science
  of anthropology in the 1860s-70s, arguing that anthropology's influence
  on Dilthey's earliest attempts to found the Geisteswissenschaften pulled
  him toward naturalistic positions contrary to the historicist hermeneutics
  of his teachers. This tension within Dilthey -- between naturalistic
  anthropological foundations and historicist interpretation -- reflects the
  ambiguity in the Herderian inheritance: Herder himself combined empirical
  naturalism about human development with cultural-historical particularism.

  RELEVANCE: Shows how the Herderian inheritance was complicated in Dilthey
  by empirical anthropology, clarifying why Dilthey's position is not simply
  continuous with Herder but represents a negotiation between rival
  tendencies in the tradition.

  POSITION: Contributes to recent scholarship reassessing Dilthey's
  relation to the natural sciences, qualifying the standard picture of
  Dilthey as straightforwardly historicist.
  },
  keywords = {dilthey, hermeneutics, geisteswissenschaften, anthropology, High},
  abstract = {Dilthey frequently recognizes anthropology as a foundational science of human nature and as a cornerstone in the system of the human sciences. While much has been written about Dilthey's "philosophical anthropology," relatively little attention has been paid to his views on the emerging empirical science of anthropology. This paper examines Dilthey's relation to the new discipline by focusing on his reception of its leading German representatives. Using his book reviews, essays, and drafts for Introduction to the Human Sciences from the 1860s-70s, it highlights the influence of the new anthropology on his earliest attempts to elaborate the foundations of the Geisteswissenschaften. It argues that anthropology was a key source for some of the naturalistic features of Dilthey's philosophy, and that it pulled him in a direction contrary to the historicist hermeneutics of his teachers.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{harrington2000objectivism,
  author = {Harrington, Austin},
  title = {Objectivism in Hermeneutics? {Gadamer}, {Habermas}, {Dilthey}},
  journal = {Philosophy of the Social Sciences},
  year = {2000},
  volume = {30},
  number = {4},
  pages = {491--507},
  doi = {10.1177/004839310003000401},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Gadamer's and Habermas's shared critique of
  Dilthey as failing to escape the "objectivism" of natural-scientific
  method because he grounds interpretive validity in the interpreter's
  capacity for complete psychological re-experiencing (Nacherleben) rather
  than in dialogical normative engagement. Harrington argues both Gadamer
  and Habermas misread Dilthey by ignoring his later, less psychologistic
  work.

  RELEVANCE: Clarifies the debates within the hermeneutic tradition about
  the adequacy of the Diltheyan -- and by extension the Herderian --
  framework for the human sciences, showing where Gadamer's reception of
  the tradition involved critical revision rather than simple continuation.

  POSITION: Partially defends Dilthey against Gadamer's and Habermas's
  critiques while acknowledging the genuine tensions in Dilthey's position.
  },
  keywords = {dilthey, gadamer, habermas, hermeneutics, objectivism, Medium},
  abstract = {Gadamer and Habermas both argue that some earlier theorists of interpretation in the human sciences, despite recognizing the meaningful character of social reality, still succumb to objectivism because they fail to conceive the relation of interpreters to their subjects in terms of cross-cultural normative “dialogue.” In particular, Gadamer and Habermas claim that the most prominent nineteenth-century philosopher of the human sciences, Wilhelm Dilthey, fell prey to a misleading Cartesian outlook which sought to ground the objectivity of interpretation on complete transcendence of the interpreter’s present cultural and historical situation. This article challenges Gadamer’s and Habermas’s claim by arguing that new research on nineteenth-century hermeneutic thought reveals Dilthey to be much more aware of the reflexive, present-centered nature of historical research than they maintain and further that their own “dialogical” model of interpretation suffers from notable obscurities which Dilthey’s work itself helps expose.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{hampton2015english,
  author = {Hampton, Alexander J. B.},
  title = {An English Source of German Romanticism: {H}erder's {C}udworth Inspired Revision of {S}pinoza from {`P}lastik{'} to {`K}raft{'}},
  journal = {The Heythrop Journal},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {58},
  number = {3},
  pages = {417--431},
  doi = {10.1111/heyj.12272},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hampton demonstrates that Herder's revision of Spinoza in Gott: Einige Gesprache (1787) drew on Ralph Cudworth's concept of "plastic nature" -- a middle principle mediating between transcendent divine reason and mechanical materialism. Herder replaced Spinoza's static substance-monism with the dynamic principle of Kraft (active force), transforming a deterministic pantheism into a teleological naturalism. This is identified as a significant but overlooked English contribution to the German intellectual tradition.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the character of Herder's Spinozism in Gott and how it was modified to accommodate teleological dynamism. The transformation from Spinoza's static God to Herder's Kraft-based God is crucial for understanding whether Herder's providentialism in the philosophy of history is genuinely teleological or merely nominally so. This article explains why Herder's modified Spinozism can support providential history in a way that Spinoza's original system cannot.

  POSITION: Stresses the un-Spinozist (Cudworthian) elements in Herder's appropriation of Spinoza, reading it as a creative revision rather than simple adoption.
  },
  keywords = {Spinozism, Kraft, Gott, Cudworth, pantheism, pantheism-controversy, High},
  abstract = {This examination considers the influence of the seventeenth century Cambridge Platonist Cudworth upon the thought of the late eighteenth century German thinker Herder. It focuses upon Herder's use of Cudworth's philosophy to create a revised version of Spinoza's metaphysics. Both Cudworth and Herder were concerned with the problem of determinism. Cudworth outlined a number of difficulties relating to this problem in the thought of Spinoza and proposed amendments, particularly the introduction of the middle principle of plastik , which would mediate between the Ideas of transcendent reason and mechanical materialism. We find these amendments to Spinoza's philosophy also employed in Herder's contribution to the Pantheism Controversy, in which he too offers a revised Spinozism and introduces his own middle principle of Kraft . This demonstrates an important but under‐explored English contribution to a key development in German intellectual history. The Pantheism Controversy was an epoch‐making event, helping to bring an end to the German Enlightenment and to inaugurate the Romantic movement. Herder's version of Spinoza's thought revived the philosopher's fortunes, and Herder's notion of Kraft became central to Romantic aesthetics. Finally, Herder's use of Cudworth demonstrates the important but overlooked source of Platonic realism in German Romantic thought.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{ankersmit2010necessity,
  author = {Ankersmit, Frank},
  title = {The Necessity of Historicism},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {4},
  number = {2},
  pages = {226--240},
  doi = {10.1163/187226310x509547},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Defends historicism as a philosophically necessary
  framework for historical understanding, arguing that the historicist
  insight -- that historical phenomena can only be understood in their own
  terms -- is not a form of relativism but the condition for genuine
  historical knowledge. Ankersmit connects this to the Herderian tradition
  in which understanding a culture or period requires entering into its own
  conceptual world rather than measuring it against transhistorical
  standards.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a philosophical defense of the historicist tradition
  that Herder inaugurated, situating his anti-universalist stance as a
  positive epistemological position with ongoing philosophical purchase.

  POSITION: Represents contemporary neo-historicism, arguing for the
  philosophical rehabilitation of historicism against positivist and
  postmodern critiques alike.
  },
  keywords = {historicism, philosophy-of-history, herder-influence, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract Rankean historicism is ordinarily seen nowadays as an outdated nineteenth century fashion and that we could not possibly tolerate in our modern intellectual homes. In opposition to this common wisdom I argue that historicism ‐ i.e. the claim that the nature of a thing is to be found in is history ‐ is no less true for all writing of history as it was in the days of Ranke. So Ranke was right, after all. I shall argue my untimely thesis by focusing on the historian’s language and by insisting that all the claims made by historicists such as Ranke make eminently sense if read as claim not about objects having existed in the past itself but about the language used by historians in their attempt to account for the past.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{forster2018influence,
  author = {Forster, Michael N.},
  title = {Intellectual Influence},
  year = {2018},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/oso/9780199588367.003.0012},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This chapter from Forster's Herder's Philosophy argues
  that Herder's intellectual influence was enormous but systematically
  underacknowledged, extending to Goethe, Schleiermacher, Schlegel,
  Schelling, Hegel, Humboldt, and Nietzsche, and to the founding of
  linguistics, anthropology, and comparative literature. The failure to
  acknowledge the debt was due to Herder's alienation from both major
  intellectual factions (Kantian and Goethean) by the early nineteenth
  century.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most comprehensive survey of Herder's downstream
  influence available in the secondary literature; the chapter is the
  logical complement to the rest of the Herder's Philosophy volume, which
  analyzes the intrinsic quality of the ideas that proved so influential.

  POSITION: Continuation of Forster's revisionist reading defending Herder's
  philosophical priority over recognized successors.
  },
  keywords = {herder-influence, romanticism, idealism, linguistics, anthropology, High},
  abstract = {This chapter argues that Herder’s intellectual influence has been enormous, not only encompassing important individual thinkers, such as Goethe, Schleiermacher, Schlegel, Schelling, Hegel, Humboldt, and Nietzsche, but also extending to the founding of whole new disciplines, including linguistics, anthropology, and comparative literature. Moreover, Herder was a sine qua non for both of the two main philosophical movements that arose in the next generation, namely German Romanticism and post-Kantian German Idealism, inspiring not only their neo-Spinozist monism but also much else in them. The failure of his successors to give him proper credit for his astonishing contributions was largely due to a simple fact: as a fiercely independent-minded critic of his contemporaries, by the beginning of the nineteenth century he had managed to alienate both of Germany’s main intellectual power blocs, namely Kant, Fichte, and their followers; and Goethe, Schiller, and theirs.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@incollection{forster2018anthropology,
  author = {Forster, Michael N.},
  title = {Philosophical Contributions to the Birth of Linguistics and Anthropology},
  booktitle = {Oxford Scholarship Online},
  year = {2018},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  doi = {10.1093/OSO/9780199588367.003.0005},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Herder's philosophical principles -- that
  language is not a transparent medium but shapes thought, that cultures
  are coherent wholes whose parts must be understood in context, that
  understanding requires imaginative empathy across cultural distance --
  were the enabling foundations for both modern linguistics (via Schlegel
  and Humboldt) and cultural anthropology (via Boas and Malinowski). The
  chapter further argues Herder's own versions of these principles were
  in important respects superior to what the disciplines developed.

  RELEVANCE: Directly traces the Herder-to-Boas transmission in detail,
  identifying the specific conceptual borrowings that make Boasian
  anthropology Herderian in philosophical foundation.

  POSITION: Extends Forster's general revisionist argument about Herder's
  influence to the specific case of anthropology, complementing Denby (2005)
  with a more detailed philosophical analysis.
  },
  keywords = {herder-boas, cultural-anthropology, linguistics, herder-influence, Medium},
  abstract = {This chapter argues that Herder contributed the fundamental philosophical principles that enabled the birth of two major new academic disciplines that we today take for granted: linguistics and cultural anthropology. In linguistics his principles were especially taken over and developed by Friedrich Schlegel and Wilhelm von Humboldt. In anthropology they were especially taken over and developed by Franz Boas and Bronislaw Malinowski, the founders of American and British anthropology, respectively. Moreover, as in some other areas (such as hermeneutics), his principles not only enabled the birth of these two new disciplines but were also in important respects even better than the versions of them that the disciplines went on to develop.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{kyslan2024kant,
  author = {Kyslan, Peter},
  title = {{Kant}, {Herder} and Anthropology},
  journal = {Folia Philosophica},
  year = {2024},
  doi = {10.31261/fp.18341},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the respective contributions of Kant and Herder to
  the emergence of cultural anthropology, arguing that Herder's opposition to
  Enlightenment universalism and his introduction of cultural relativism --
  the insistence on the uniqueness and dignity of different cultures --
  shaped the distinctively cultural approach to anthropology that later
  developed in the Boasian school, while Kant's transcendental idealism
  contributed a universal-structural framework with different implications.

  RELEVANCE: Situates Herder's contribution to anthropology in relation to
  Kant's rival project, clarifying what is distinctively Herderian in the
  cultural anthropological tradition; useful for the domain's focus on the
  Herder-Boas transmission.

  POSITION: Represents the ongoing scholarly effort to differentiate Herder's
  from Kant's anthropological legacy, complementing longer treatments by
  Zammito and Forster.
  },
  keywords = {herder-boas, kant, cultural-anthropology, cultural-relativism, Low},
  abstract = {This study explores the philosophical contributions of Immanuel Kant and Johann Gottfried Herder to the field of cultural anthropology. It examines Kant’s idealism and transcendentalism, which provided a universal perspective on culture and influenced various anthropological concepts. The research addresses the criticisms of Kant’s views on race and gender, highlighting the evolution of his thoughts in response to emerging scientific disciplines. Additionally, the study delves into Herder’s role in shaping modern cultural studies, emphasizing his opposition to Enlightenment universalism and his introduction of cultural relativism. Herder’s ideas on the uniqueness and dignity of different cultures are analyzed, illustrating their impact on the development of cultural anthropology. The paper concludes with the discussion of the ongoing relevance of Kant’s and Herder’s philosophies in contemporary anthropological discourse.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{schmidtzammito2003review,
  author = {Schmidt, Claudia M. and Zammito, John H.},
  title = {{Kant, Herder, and the Birth of Anthropology}},
  journal = {The German Quarterly},
  year = {2003},
  volume = {76},
  number = {2},
  pages = {221},
  doi = {10.2307/3252180},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This item is a review article whose DOI resolves to a
  review of Zammito's 2002 book (University of Chicago Press); the book
  itself argues that the Kant-Herder controversy over anthropology and
  the philosophy of history was the crucible in which modern concepts of
  culture, race, and human nature were formed, tracing the philosophical
  conflict between Kant's universalist anthropology and Herder's historicist
  particularism and following the transmission of Herderian themes into
  twentieth-century Boasian anthropology.

  RELEVANCE: The Zammito book is a foundational text for the Herder-to-Boas
  transmission argument and directly addresses how the Herder-Kant debate
  shaped the conceptual inheritance available to Boas.

  POSITION: Represents the historiographical approach that links the origins
  of modern anthropology to the Kant-Herder philosophical dispute, situating
  Boasian cultural anthropology as the resolution of problems first posed by
  Herder.
  },
  keywords = {herder-boas, kant, cultural-anthropology, herder-hegel, Low},
  abstract = {Zammito, John H. Kant, Herder, and the Birth of Anthropology. Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 2002. 576 pp. $68.00 hardcover; $29.00 paperback. In this prequel to his The Genesis of Kant's Critique of Judgment, Zammito offers a compelling account of one of the most significant divergences in eighteenth century German thought: the breach between Kant and his student Herder over the relation of philosophy to anthropology, or the study of human nature. He presents the intellectual biographies of Kant and Herder as two trajectories which converged from roughly 1762-1767 and then diverged, sharply dividing the intellectual world then and subsequently. Zammito argues that the personal acquaintance between Kant and Herder coincided with a distinctive and underappreciated phase in Kant's career. In the early 1760s, Kant rejected the rationalist philosophy of the Wolffian tradition, in favor of the empiricism of the British philosophers and Rousseau. At the same time, he developed misgivings about the established role of a university professor as too isolated from the needs of an emerging German cultured class. He accordingly refashioned himself for a brief time as a Popularphilosophe. In this persona, Kant offered his students, including Herder, new intellectual vistas arising from empirical philosophy, including an anthropological study of the human mind and of history and culture. However, by 1768 Kant had begun to see new possibilities for a rigorous philosophy founded on rationalist principles, and to react against any signs of facile dilettantism in philosophy. During this new dogmatic turn, he offered a lukewarm response to Herder's early writings, thereby initiating a lifelong intellectual and personal breach between them. By the early 1770s, when Herder was reconceiving philosophy as an anthropological discipline, Kant was pursuing his own interest in anthropology as an empirical and pragmatic sideline, especially by developing a new course in anthropology at the University of Konigsberg: a survival of his ambitions as a popular philosopher, but rigorously separated from his philosophical work. In spite of its considerable strengths as a work of biography and intellectual history, Zammito's study deals only superficially with the main philosophical issue in the later divergence between Kant and Herder: the relationship of anthropology to philosophy, or more precisely of Herder's hermeneutic historicism to Kant's critical philosophy. …},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{crowe2012herder,
  author = {Crowe, Benjamin D.},
  title = {Herder's Moral Philosophy: Perfectionism, Sentimentalism and Theism},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {20},
  number = {6},
  pages = {1141--1161},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2012.731243},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Crowe argues that Herder's moral philosophy combines three elements -- perfectionism (the idea that human beings have a nature that is to be developed), sentimentalism (moral motivation through feeling and sympathy), and theism (the perfectionist telos is grounded in God's providential order). The theological element is not incidental but constitutive: Herder's perfectionism requires a divine framework to give the developmental ideal its normative force and to explain why human nature tends toward Humanitat.

  RELEVANCE: This article is essential for understanding how Herder's theology connects to his normative philosophy of history. Crowe's demonstration that perfectionism requires theism directly addresses the question of whether Herder's Gott-period Spinozism underwrites or undermines the providential structure of the Ideen. It also clarifies what kind of telos Humanitat is: not a free-floating regulative ideal but a theistically grounded perfectionist end.

  POSITION: Defends a theistic-perfectionist reading of Herder's ethics against purely secular or naturalistic interpretations.
  },
  keywords = {moral-philosophy, perfectionism, theism, providence, Humanitat, High},
  abstract = {While the last several decades have seen a renaissance of scholarship on J. G. Herder (1744–1804), his moral philosophy has not been carefully examined. The aim of this paper is to fill this gap, and to point the way for further research, by reconstructing his original and systematically articulated views on morality. Three interrelated elements of his position are explored in detail: (1) his perfectionism, or theory of the human good; (2) his sentimentalism, which includes moral epistemology and a theory of moral education; and (3) his theism, which deepens and justifies these other elements.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@incollection{forster2012herder,
  author = {Forster, Michael N.},
  title = {Herder and {S}pinoza},
  booktitle = {Spinoza and German Idealism},
  editor = {F{\"o}rster, Eckart and Melamed, Yitzhak Y.},
  year = {2012},
  pages = {59--84},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi = {10.1017/cbo9781139135139.005},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Forster argues that the flourishing of Spinozism in late eighteenth-century Germany owes more to Herder than to Jacobi or Mendelssohn, whose famous Pantheismusstreit debate is usually credited. Herder's version of Spinoza introduces genuine developmental and teleological elements absent from Spinoza himself, making Herder's "Spinozism" a transformation that enables rather than precludes a philosophy of historical development grounded in divine immanence.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the precise character of Herder's engagement with Spinoza in the context of the Pantheism Controversy, which is background for understanding how Gott relates to the Ideen. Forster's argument that Herder's Spinozism incorporates teleology directly addresses the question of how the theological dimension informs Herder's providentialist philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Argues for Herder's creative and transformative (not merely derivative) use of Spinoza, stressing the developmental and teleological innovations.
  },
  keywords = {Spinozism, Gott, Pantheism-Controversy, German-idealism, providence, High},
  abstract = {A summary is not available for this content so a preview has been provided. Please use the Get access link above for information on how to access this content.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{solanki2024poesis,
  author = {Solanki, Tanvi},
  title = {Poesis, {G}od, and the Connectedness of All Beings},
  journal = {Historical Reflections/R{\'e}flexions Historiques},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {50},
  number = {1},
  pages = {1--21},
  doi = {10.3167/hrrh.2024.500101},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Solanki examines Herder's analogical thinking as emerging from his Spinozist concept of God and nature, showing that Herder's comparative method posits phenomena as instantiating a common divine-natural creative power (poesis) in their own singular manner. This method resists both assimilation of differences and static hierarchies, and was influential on Goethe. Solanki argues it has been overlooked because it resists standard historicist periodization.

  RELEVANCE: Connects Herder's theological-metaphysical framework (the Spinozist God of Gott) to his methodology for understanding cultural and natural diversity -- directly relevant to the question of how providentialism and particular-cultural worth are related. The argument that Herder's comparative method refuses hierarchy without flattening difference illuminates how the telos of Humanitat functions without imposing a ranked hierarchy of cultures.

  POSITION: Argues that Herder's analogical method, grounded in his Spinozist theology, provides a framework for non-hierarchical cross-cultural comparison.
  },
  keywords = {analogy, Spinozism, comparative-method, Gott, poesis, Medium},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
The article examines the significance of J. G. Herder's analogical thinking in the history of comparative practices. It shows how Herder's comparative method, of positing one phenomenon as another in its own singular and inimitable manner, emerges from his concept of God and nature as informed by Baruch Spinoza, as well as his study of scientific and cultural phenomena, including the active mechanisms of poesis as sensory perception. While Herder's concept of comparison and his analogical practices influenced prominent thinkers contemporary to him such as J.W. Goethe, the specificity of his comparative method has been overlooked by scholars past and present, such as Michel Foucault, as Herder's approach resists assimilation in histories of comparativism centered on nineteenth-century projects of comparison. Yet his outlook on the tension between difference and identity offers fresh insight into relations between distinct cultures and entities without effacing their particularity or placing them in static hierarchies. Herder's comparative method provides new perspectives on contemporary debates on the benefits of and dangers of historical analogies.},
}

@article{layne2023herder,
  author = {Layne, Priscilla and Norberg, Jakob},
  title = {Herder and the Black Slave},
  journal = {Monatshefte},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {115},
  number = {4},
  pages = {553--572},
  doi = {10.3368/m.115.4.553},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Layne and Norberg argue that Herder's "Negro Idylls" in the tenth collection of the Briefe zur Beförderung der Humanität are politically revealing: Herder's hope for the future redemption of humanity requires that Black slaves renounce retribution for historical wrongs, making his concept of Humanitat structurally dependent on the self-effacement of the oppressed. Despite his genuine anti-slavery commitments, Herder's universal humanity is thus "shored up" by a conception of the Black subject that denies full humanity.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a critical perspective on what Humanitat actually entails in the Briefe, Herder's most sustained late treatment of the concept. The argument that Humanitat's universalism conceals a structural exclusion is directly relevant to the question of whether a universal normative telos can coexist with genuine cultural particularity and whether Herder's providentialism is egalitarian or implicitly hierarchical.

  POSITION: Critical reading: Humanitat's universalism contains a structural contradiction when confronted with the reality of racialized oppression.
  },
  keywords = {Humanitat, Briefe, race, slavery, universalism, critique, Medium},
  abstract = {We argue that Herder’s often-ignored “Negro Idylls” in the tenth and final collection of his Briefe zu Beförderung der Humanität are both politically significant and highly revealing of the limitations of Herder’s political philosophy. The poems’ compassionate but politically confining representations of Black slaves ultimately seek to resolve the internal tensions of his own account of “humanity,” the topic of the entire tenth collection, and indeed of the entire work. Our reading shows how the “Negro Idylls” reveal that the structure of Herder’s political philosophy is shored up and rendered coherent only by a particular conception of the Black slave as a non-avenger, a figure who willingly renounces retribution for historical wrongs to facilitate an otherwise threatened future redemption of humanity. Despite Herder’s genuine commitment to the abolition of slavery and his scathing critique of colonialism, then, Herder’s hope for humanity as a whole rests on the shoulders of Black subjects, even as it deprives these subjects of their own humanity. (PL/JN)},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{englander2013herder,
  author = {Englander, Alex},
  title = {Herder's {`E}xpressivist{'} Metaphysics and the Origins of {G}erman {I}dealism},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {21},
  number = {5},
  pages = {902--924},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2013.805120},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Englander examines Herder's "expressivist" metaphysics -- the idea that reality consists of forces that express themselves through natural and cultural forms -- and argues that this framework is a significant but underappreciated source for German Idealism. Herder's claim that force expresses itself through particular forms without being exhausted by any of them provides a model for understanding how a universal divine power can manifest through the irreducible particularity of cultures.

  RELEVANCE: Connects Herder's Spinozist/Kraft metaphysics to his philosophy of history: if the divine force expressing itself through history is identified with Humanitat, then expressivist metaphysics provides the ontological grounding for why universal and particular are not in tension but mutually constituting. This article clarifies how Herder's theology in Gott supports rather than undermines the pluralism of the Ideen.

  POSITION: Situates Herder's metaphysics as expressivist rather than simply Spinozist, linking it to the genesis of German Idealism.
  },
  keywords = {expressivist-metaphysics, Kraft, German-idealism, Spinozism, Low},
  abstract = {AbstractCharles Taylor's influential exposition of Hegel made the doctrine of expressivism of central importance and identified Herder as its exemplary historical advocate. The breadth and generality of Taylor's use of ‘expressivism’ have led the concept into some disrepute, but a more precise formulation of the doctrine as a theory of meaning can both demonstrate what is worthwhile and accurate in Taylor's account, and allow us a useful point of entry into Herder's multifaceted philosophy. A reconstruction of Herder's overall philosophical position, centred around a refined theory of what this paper labels ‘Herderean expressivism’, reveals a naturalistic, teleological metaphysics. This metaphysics fulfils the Hegelian aim of providing what Dieter Henrich has called a ‘feedback loop’ between ontology and epistemology. Exploring Herder's expressivism, therefore, helps further the case for his decisive impact on Hegel's philosophy. Herder's methodological naturalism, however, represents an obstacle to Hegel's absolute idealism.Keywords: J. G. HerderG. W. F. Hegelexpressivism Notes1 A helpful guide to the politics of these disputes can be found in Hacking, Historical Ontology, 121–40. Berlin, it should be noted, celebrates Herder's cosmopolitanism and humanism. Yet he believes what he calls Herder's ‘pluralism’ slides into cultural relativism and finds its ‘consequences’ in Romantic anti-rationalism and nationalism. See Berlin, Vico and Herder, 212–3.2 In this paper, I concentrate upon Hegel, although an analogous account could certainly be given for Schelling. For some details of the Herder-Schelling relation, see Beiser, German Idealism.3 There is a long scholarly tradition emphasizing the decisive influence of Herder upon Hegel. See Harris, Hegel's Development. What Hegel read of Herder is difficult to ascertain. Most importantly, we know he wrote a (now missing) review of the two editions of Herder's dialogue Gott: Einige Gespräche [henceforth God] of 1787 and 1800. Hegel refers to several of Herder's writings on the Old Testament in his early theological essays and later lectures on religion, and comments upon his philosophy in the 1802/1803 Glauben und Wissen. There are abundant signs that he was familiar with several of Herder's central works, in particular his Vom Erkennen und Empfinden der Menschlichen Seele [henceforth On the Cognition] and his pioneering work in the philosophy of history, the Ideen zur Philosophie der Geschichte der Menscheit [henceforth Ideas]. For a detailed examination, see Forster, Hegel's Idea of a Phenomenology.4 This limited reconstruction of Herder's philosophy certainly omits some of the ideas which were most relevant to his philosophical successors, especially his ‘hylozoism’ or vitalism, which was crucial both for the idealists' epistemological questions and for the development of Naturphilosophie. For these topics, see Nisbet, Herder and Scientific Thought; Zammito, ‘Epigenesis’, 131–45.5 Hacking, Why Does Language Matter, 15–43, offers a good account of how this characterization applies to Hobbes, the Port Royal Logic, Locke and Berkeley, all representatives of the ‘way of ideas’.6 This characterization of ‘crude empiricist designativism’ is indebted to Rosen. Taylor's presentation of the object of expressivist attack agrees with these particulars. He explains how pre-expressivist theories involve an ontological division between semantic and non-semantic entities, with the former gaining their semantic properties through merely ‘external relations’ of reference to the latter. These relations are established ‘subjectively’, and therefore exist in the mind alone.7 Herder, unlike certain later romantics, does not regard the coalescence of form and matter as an a priori obstacle to successful translation. His own theory of translation contains several innovative methods for recapturing the tones and senses of original works. For a helpful account of Herder's theory, see Ulrich Gaier's commentary to Herder's Volkslieder, especially W3: 909–18. References to Herder's German texts are cited by ‘W’ followed by volume and page number, and taken from Herder, Werke. Translations of Herder's writings are from Philosophical Writings, henceforth cited by ‘PW’ and page number, and Selected Writings on Aesthetics, cited by ‘Aesthetics’ and page number. Translations from Gott: Einige Gespräche are from God: Some Conversations.8 See Irmscher, ‘Beobachtungen zur Funktion der Analogie im Denken Herders’, 64–98, 65–75 for discussion.9 See Nisbet for an extended discussion.10 See Clark, ‘Herder's Conception of “Kraft”’, 737–52, for details.11 As Marion Heinz points out, sensations [Empfindungen] in Herder refer both to a system of bodily states or low-grade forces of representation (in Leibnizian fashion) and to representations of the soul on the other mediated through the body. The latter, cognitive, capacity is inseparable from the former. See Heinz, ‘Grundzüge von Herders Psychologie’, 137–52, 142.12 Cf. Herder, PW, 210–11, One will never get to bottom of these forces [i.e. mental capacities] if one merely treats them superficially as ideas that dwell in the soul, or, worse still, separates them from one another as walled compartments and considers them individually in independence.13 The many subordinate forces of the soul such as imagination, foresight and memory must not be seen as separated off from one another and as separate powers, but as at bottom a single force that is ‘understanding, intuition with inner consciousness’ or ‘inner activity that looks within itself, consciousness, apperception’ PW, 210–11.14 See Beiser, The Fate of Reason, 145ff. In the 1775 draft of On the Cognition, Herder states that ‘Psychology must at every step be physiology’. In this essay, Herder wants to base all sensation, and by extension all thought, on one purely physiological manifestation of force, Haller's discovery of ‘irritability’ [Reizbarkeit]. Psychology is equally physiology since ‘Sensation is only the aggregate of all obscure irritations, just as thought is the luminous aggregate of sensation.’ Herder, PW, 197. In the same draft, Herder explains how whilst there is a continuity of laws in sensation and cognition, they are present in the latter in a ‘higher equation’ and the ‘string-play of an irritation … now communicates itself and takes effect in an infinitely more mentalistic, higher way’ PW, 201.15 Irmscher, 86. Cf. Aesthetics 359: ‘Human cognition starts from the senses and from experience, and everything comes back to them.’16 Gaier, ‘The problem…’, 301 goes so far as to say that the notion of ‘harmonisation’ or ‘coherence’ functions as a ‘transcendental principle’ for Herder.17 In stark contrast to Hume, Herder believes that our activities of observation, abstraction, combination, and inference are, though not infallible, inner necessities of thought which derive from divine necessity. They are activities standing in analogous relation to the organizational principles governing groupings of forces throughout the cosmos. Hence, Herder states that this necessary basis of thought is to the world of minds what equilibrium is to the world of bodies. As such, they possess a qualified universality; a being with finer senses would still think according to the same unique necessary divine ‘laws’. Cf. God, 149–50 and PW, 208.18 PW, 215: ‘true cognition and good volition are just one sort of thing, a single force and efficacy of the soul.’19 See the discussion at W1:144.20 For further discussion, see Stern, ‘Kant, Hegel, and the Place of the Subject’.21 This meaning of subjectivism is at issue in the discussion of cultural absolutism versus relativism in Spencer, Herder's Political Thought, chap. 4. Spencer contends that Herder charts a sophisticated middle course she calls ‘weak pluralism’.22 This form of subjectivism, criticised earlier as an element of Taylor's expressivism, can nourish a version of (2), especially an aesthetic version: putatively ‘objective’ standards cannot do justice to the unique particularity of artworks given their role as expressions of the artist's irreducible subjectivity. Indeed, many commentators criticize this element of Taylor's interpretation precisely for its association of Herder with elements of later Romanticism. See Spencer, Herder's Political Thought, 42–3, and Dallmayr, ‘Truth and Diversity’, 101–24, 107–9. I agree with Spencer, however, that Dallmayr throws the baby out with the bathwater by rejecting the term expressivism altogether.23 Whilst correctly distancing Herder from scepticism and idealism (‘fully-fledged scepticism’), Sikka agrees that for both Kant and Herder our fundamental concepts are ‘subjective’ in being inapplicable to ‘things in themselves’. Sikka, 205.24 I thank Raymond Geuss, Michael Forster, Gabriel Roberts and two anonymous referees for the journal for their helpful comments on various versions of this essay.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

